ML_PAGE("l4-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 4 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l4\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Establish the principle</b><p>Define the concept accurately in the chapter's terms: time value of money, total return, required return, HPR, IRR, risk, standard deviation, CV.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Show <em>why</em> the relationship exists: the economic cause, not just the result.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply and analyse</b><p>Connect it to the scenario: calculate (FV, PV, HPR, IRR, σ, CV) with formula and substitution, then compare with the required return.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate and conclude</b><p>Weigh alternatives, name a limitation or assumption, and give a justified, conditional conclusion.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Chapter 4 + 4A answers combine calculation with judgment: a number is never the conclusion until it is compared with the required return or the right alternative.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate whether a 15% expected return makes an investment attractive.</p>\n        <p>\"A 15% return is attractive because it is a high return and would allow investors to earn more profit.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Treats a number as good in isolation; never mentions risk or required return.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"An investment's attractiveness cannot be judged from its expected return alone. Under the risk-return trade-off, the expected return must be compared with the required return, which combines the risk-free rate and a risk premium. If the required return is 18%, an expected 15% is insufficient compensation despite looking high; if it is 10%, the same investment may be acceptable. The conclusion also depends on how reliable the expected cash flows are and on the investor's ability to bear unfavourable outcomes. So 15% must be judged relative to its risk.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Principle + mechanism + application with numbers + conditional evaluation.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>Investment returns and performance evaluation (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor is evaluating two securities over a one-year holding period.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Information</th>\n<th>Investment A</th>\n<th>Investment B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beginning share price</td>\n<td>RM100</td>\n<td>RM80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ending share price</td>\n<td>RM112</td>\n<td>RM75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Income received</td>\n<td>RM3</td>\n<td>RM9</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor claims that Investment B is superior because it provides three times as much cash income.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the capital gain or loss, total return in RM and holding-period return (HPR) for both investments.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Distinguish income return, capital gain or loss, realized return and paper return.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Critically evaluate the investor's claim and explain the limitations of relying solely on historical investment returns.</p>\n<p>(d) [5 marks] Analyse how internal characteristics and external economic forces may affect future returns.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 1</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Capital gain, total return and HPR</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines total return as the sum of income and capital gain or loss over a specified period.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Capital Gain/Loss}=P_1-P_0$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Total Return}=Income+(P_1-P_0)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR=\\frac{Income+(P_1-P_0)}{P_0}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Investment A</strong></p>\n<p>Capital gain:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}112-\\text{RM}100=\\text{RM}12$$</div>\n<p>Total return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}3+\\text{RM}12=\\boxed{\\text{RM}15}$$</div>\n<p>Holding-period return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$HPR_A=\\frac{3+12}{100}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR_A=15\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Investment B</strong></p>\n<p>Capital loss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}75-\\text{RM}80=-\\text{RM}5$$</div>\n<p>Total return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}9-\\text{RM}5=\\boxed{\\text{RM}4}$$</div>\n<p>Holding-period return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$HPR_B=\\frac{9-5}{80}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR_B=5\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>A</th>\n<th>B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Income</td>\n<td>RM3</td>\n<td>RM9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital gain/loss</td>\n<td>+RM12</td>\n<td>-RM5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total return</td>\n<td>RM15</td>\n<td>RM4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HPR</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 3 marks per investment for the capital gain/loss, total return and HPR.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Return components and realization</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Income return represents cash or near-cash received from owning an investment, such as dividends and interest.</p>\n<p>Capital gain or loss represents the difference between the investment's selling proceeds and original purchase price. When shares are still held, changes in market value can be included in a holding-period valuation, but the gains or losses have not been realized through sale.</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes realized returns from paper returns.</p>\n<p>A realized return has been actually received by the investor, while a paper return reflects an increase or decrease in investment value that has not yet been realized through disposal.</p>\n<p>For example, if Investment A has not been sold, the RM12 increase in its share value represents an unrealized capital gain, while its RM3 dividend has already been received.</p>\n<p>If the investor sells the shares at RM112, the capital gain becomes realized.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluation of Investment B</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The claim that Investment B is superior because it provides more income is incomplete.</p>\n<p>Investment B produces RM9 of income compared with RM3 for Investment A. However, it simultaneously suffers a RM5 capital loss.</p>\n<p>Consequently, B's total return is only RM4, equivalent to 5% of its initial value.</p>\n<p>Investment A generates a higher total return of 15% because its capital appreciation more than compensates for its lower dividend income.</p>\n<p>Therefore, A performed better over the historical period according to HPR.</p>\n<p>However, this does not prove that A will be the superior investment in the future.</p>\n<p>The chapter explicitly states that historical returns provide a basis for future expectations but do not guarantee future performance.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the data do not indicate the uncertainty surrounding future returns.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investment A delivered stronger historical total-return performance, but the investor should assess its expected return, risks and required return before making a future investment decision.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Internal and external factors</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies both internal characteristics and external forces affecting investment returns.</p>\n<p>Internal characteristics include the investment type, issuer management and the issuer's financing arrangements.</p>\n<p>For example, poor management decisions may reduce profitability, while excessive borrowing can create additional financial obligations.</p>\n<p>External forces include political, business and economic conditions, inflation and deflation.</p>\n<p>For example, a recession may weaken demand and corporate earnings, while inflation can reduce the purchasing power of investment income.</p>\n<p>These factors may change future investment values even when historical performance was strong.</p>\n<p>Therefore, past returns should be analysed alongside company characteristics and broader economic conditions.</p>\n<p>Why this earns high marks: The answer distinguishes income from total return, demonstrates the calculations, evaluates the historical comparison and recognizes the uncertainty surrounding future performance.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>Time value of money and required return (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A company offers an investment opportunity requiring RM12,000 immediately.</p>\n<p>The investment is expected to generate the following end-of-year cash flows:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Expected cash flow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM4,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM5,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM6,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An investor estimates a real required return component of 3%, an expected inflation premium of 2% and an investment-specific risk premium of 5%.</p>\n<p>(a) [5 marks] Explain the concept of time value of money and why future investment benefits must be discounted.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate the risk-free rate and required return using the chapter's model.</p>\n<p>(c) [7 marks] Calculate the present value of the expected benefits and determine whether the investment satisfies the chapter's satisfactory-investment rule.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate two important assumptions that could change the investment decision.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objectives 2 and 3 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Time value of money</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The time value of money states that money received today is generally worth more than the same nominal amount received in the future, provided money can earn a positive return.</p>\n<p>This occurs because funds received earlier can be invested and generate additional income.</p>\n<p>Consequently, investment benefits received at different dates cannot be compared directly.</p>\n<p>To evaluate whether an investment is satisfactory, the investor must convert future benefits into their present values using an appropriate discount rate.</p>\n<p>The chapter defines a satisfactory investment as one where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ PV(\\text{Benefits})\\ge PV(\\text{Costs}) }$$</div>\n<p>This rule ensures that the investment's benefits adequately compensate the investor for committing funds over time.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Risk-free and required returns</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter uses the additive relationship:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=R_{\\text{real}}+IP$$</div>\n<p>where $IP$ is the expected inflation premium.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=3\\%+2\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_f=5\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The required return also incorporates an investment-specific risk premium:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{required}}=R_f+RP$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=5\\%+5\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{required}}=10\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The risk-free rate compensates the investor for the real return and expected inflation components under the textbook's model.</p>\n<p>The additional risk premium compensates the investor for the risks associated with the investment and its issuer.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Present value and investment acceptability</strong> [7 marks]</p>\n<p>The present value of the benefits is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$$</div>\n<p>Using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>Year 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_1=\\frac{4{,}000}{1.10}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}3{,}636.36$$</div>\n<p>Year 2:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_2=\\frac{5{,}000}{1.10^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}4{,}132.23$$</div>\n<p>Year 3:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_3=\\frac{6{,}000}{1.10^3}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}4{,}507.89$$</div>\n<p>Total present value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{benefits}} = 3{,}636.36+4{,}132.23+4{,}507.89$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{benefits}}\\approx \\text{RM}12{,}276.48}$$</div>\n<p>Initial cost:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{cost}}=\\text{RM}12{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The difference is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NPV=12{,}276.48-12{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{NPV=+\\text{RM}276.48}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>PV of expected benefits</th>\n<th>Investment cost</th>\n<th>NPV</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM12,276.48</td>\n<td>RM12,000</td>\n<td><strong>+RM276.48</strong> (positive: exceeds the required compensation)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV(\\text{Benefits})&gt;PV(\\text{Cost})$$</div>\n<p>the investment is satisfactory under the chapter's present-value rule and the stated assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating assumptions</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>First, the projected cash flows may be uncertain.</p>\n<p>If the company fails to generate the expected RM4,000, RM5,000 and RM6,000 payments, the actual investment benefits could fall below the estimated present value.</p>\n<p>Second, the 10% required return depends on the assessed risk premium and expected inflation.</p>\n<p>If the investment proves riskier than originally estimated, the investor may require a higher return.</p>\n<p>Increasing the discount rate would reduce the present value of the expected future benefits and could reverse the decision.</p>\n<p>The positive NPV is also relatively small compared with the initial RM12,000 cost, so the investment has limited room for adverse changes in assumptions.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The investment is acceptable using the estimated 10% required return, but management should test the reliability of its cash-flow forecast and risk assessment before approval.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>A positive NPV is a conditional valuation conclusion. It does not guarantee that the future cash flows will actually occur.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>HPR, IRR and dividend growth (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investment analyst reviews four opportunities.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Investment A: A stock purchased for RM50 is worth RM57 one year later and has paid RM3 in dividends.</li>\n<li>Investment B: An asset costing RM1,000 is expected to be worth RM1,400 after five years, with no interim income.</li>\n<li>Investment C: A company's dividend increased from RM0.92 to RM1.85 over nine years.</li>\n<li>Investment D: An investment costs RM1,000 today and is expected to pay RM600 at the end of Year 1 and RM600 at the end of Year 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Assume Investment D has a required return of 12%.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Calculate and interpret Investment A's HPR.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate Investment B's compound annual yield and explain why its five-year total return should not be confused with an annual return.</p>\n<p>(c) [3 marks] Calculate Investment C's compound annual dividend growth rate.</p>\n<p>(d) [6 marks] Calculate Investment D's IRR and evaluate its acceptability under the chapter's decision rule.</p>\n<p>(e) [3 marks] Explain the interest-on-interest and reinvestment-rate assumption discussed in the chapter.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Investment A: HPR</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The beginning share price is RM50, ending value is RM57 and dividend income is RM3.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$HPR=\\frac{3+(57-50)}{50}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac{10}{50}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR=20\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The investment generated a total return of RM10 per share, comprising RM3 income and RM7 capital appreciation.</p>\n<p>This represents 20% of its initial value over the one-year holding period.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Investment B: Compound annual yield</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The asset costs RM1,000 and is expected to be worth RM1,400 after five years.</p>\n<p>The cumulative return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{1{,}400-1{,}000}{1{,}000}=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>However, the 40% return occurs over five years.</p>\n<p>To determine its compound annual yield:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=PV(1+r)^n$$</div>\n<p>Rearranging:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=\\left(\\frac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n}-1$$</div>\n<p>Substituting:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=\\left(\\frac{1{,}400}{1{,}000}\\right)^{1/5}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{r\\approx6.96\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The investment earns a compound annual return of approximately 6.96%, not 40% annually.</p>\n<p>The distinction matters because HPR can mislead investors when comparing investments held for different lengths of time.</p>\n<p>The chapter explicitly identifies this limitation and recommends yield or IRR for longer holding periods.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investment C: Dividend growth</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>Beginning dividend:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D_0=\\text{RM}0.92$$</div>\n<p>Ending dividend:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D_n=\\text{RM}1.85$$</div>\n<p>Number of years:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=9$$</div>\n<p>The compound annual dividend growth rate is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$g=\\left(\\frac{D_n}{D_0}\\right)^{1/n}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$g=\\left(\\frac{1.85}{0.92}\\right)^{1/9}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{g\\approx8.07\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This indicates that the dividend increased at an equivalent compound annual rate of approximately 8.07% over the historical period.</p>\n<p>It does not guarantee that future dividends will grow at the same rate.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Investment D: IRR and acceptability</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investment requires RM1,000 today and produces RM600 at the end of both Years 1 and 2.</p>\n<p>IRR is the discount rate that equates the present value of the future cash-flow stream with the investment's cost.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1{,}000=\\frac{600}{(1+IRR)}+\\frac{600}{(1+IRR)^2}$$</div>\n<p>Solving gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{IRR\\approx13.07\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The required return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{required}}=12\\%$$</div>\n<p>Compare:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IRR=13.07\\%&gt;12\\%$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, the investment exceeds the required return by approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$13.07\\%-12\\%=1.07\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<p>Under the chapter's decision rule:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{IRR\\ge R_{\\text{required}}\\Rightarrow\\text{Acceptable}}$$</div>\n<p>Investment D is acceptable under the stated cash-flow and discount-rate assumptions.</p>\n<p>However, this result does not guarantee that the actual investment return will equal the estimated IRR.</p>\n<p><strong>(e) Interest-on-interest and reinvestment</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter explains that interest on interest occurs when investment income received during the holding period is reinvested and earns additional income.</p>\n<p>The reinvestment rate is the rate earned on the interest or other income received from an investment.</p>\n<p>The chapter associates its fully compounded interpretation of yield with the assumption that interim income is reinvested at the same rate as the original investment's IRR.</p>\n<p>If interim income can only be reinvested at a lower rate, the investor may accumulate less terminal wealth than that assumption suggests.</p>\n<p>A technical distinction is important: the IRR equation itself calculates a discount rate equating investment cost and cash flows. Reinvesting distributions at the IRR becomes relevant when using that rate to describe or project a fully compounded terminal investment outcome.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: HPR, IRR and dividend growth measure different things. HPR measures total return over a holding period, IRR accounts for the timing of cash flows, and dividend growth measures the compound rate of change in a stream of income.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>Identifying and evaluating investment risks (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A diversified investment company is facing the following developments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One operating subsidiary is losing market share because of poor management decisions.</li>\n<li>Another subsidiary relies heavily on debt and is struggling to meet interest obligations.</li>\n<li>Inflation increases while the company holds fixed-income securities.</li>\n<li>Market interest rates rise, reducing the value of existing fixed-rate bonds.</li>\n<li>A property must be sold quickly at a substantial discount.</li>\n<li>An unfavorable change in taxation reduces the after-tax benefit of an investment.</li>\n<li>A major hurricane unexpectedly damages insured properties.</li>\n<li>A broad economic downturn causes many securities to decline.</li>\n<li>Foreign-currency movements reduce the domestic value of international investments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(a) [9 marks] Identify and explain the nine sources of risk illustrated in the scenario.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Differentiate business risk from financial risk, and event risk from market risk.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Evaluate why identifying risk categories is necessary but insufficient for selecting a suitable investment. Recommend how the company should incorporate these risks into its investment assessment.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Identifying the nine sources of risk</strong> [9 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies nine major sources of risk. Each concerns a different mechanism through which an investment's return or value can be adversely affected.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Company loses market share due to poor management</td>\n<td>Business risk</td>\n<td>Uncertainty surrounding business earnings and the ability to generate returns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Highly indebted company struggles to meet interest obligations</td>\n<td>Financial risk</td>\n<td>Uncertainty arising from the firm's debt and equity financing structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inflation increases while fixed nominal income remains unchanged</td>\n<td>Purchasing-power risk</td>\n<td>Changes in price levels reduce the real value of investment returns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rising interest rates reduce existing bond prices</td>\n<td>Interest-rate risk</td>\n<td>Changes in market interest rates adversely affect security values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Property must be sold at a substantial discount</td>\n<td>Liquidity risk</td>\n<td>Inability to convert an investment conveniently into cash at a reasonable price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tax-law changes reduce investment benefits</td>\n<td>Tax risk</td>\n<td>Unfavorable tax changes reduce after-tax returns and potentially market values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A hurricane damages insured properties</td>\n<td>Event risk</td>\n<td>An unexpected event produces a significant and unusually immediate financial effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broad economic deterioration causes market declines</td>\n<td>Market risk</td>\n<td>Market-wide factors affect investment returns independently of a particular issuer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Foreign-currency depreciation reduces domestic investment value</td>\n<td>Currency exchange risk</td>\n<td>Exchange-rate changes alter returns when foreign investments are valued in the domestic currency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 1 mark per correctly identified and explained risk category.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Differentiating related risks</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>Business risk versus financial risk</p>\n<p>Business risk concerns uncertainty surrounding a company's operations and earnings.</p>\n<p>For example, ineffective management or declining market share may reduce revenue and profitability.</p>\n<p>Financial risk concerns uncertainty arising from the company's capital structure, particularly the proportion of debt financing.</p>\n<p>A company may operate profitably but remain financially vulnerable because of large debt obligations.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Business Risk}=\\text{Operating/Earnings Uncertainty}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Financial Risk}=\\text{Financing-Related Uncertainty}}$$</div>\n<p>These risks are related but not identical. Poor operating performance can make debt obligations harder to service, causing both risks to interact.</p>\n<p>Event risk versus market risk</p>\n<p>Event risk arises from unexpected events producing significant and unusually immediate effects on investments.</p>\n<p>A hurricane that causes substantial insurance claims is an example given in the chapter.</p>\n<p>Market risk arises from broader factors, including political upheaval and changing economic conditions, which can affect securities across the market.</p>\n<p>A broad market decline may affect numerous firms simultaneously, whereas an event may have a more direct impact on a particular issuer.</p>\n<p>However, a severe event can also have wider market consequences. These classifications help identify risk mechanisms; they do not mean real-world events can affect only one category.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluation and recommended assessment</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Identifying risk categories is necessary because investors must understand what could cause an investment to perform differently from expectations.</p>\n<p>However, identification alone is insufficient to determine whether an investment is suitable.</p>\n<p>Two investments may both face business risk, yet differ substantially in the uncertainty of their earnings.</p>\n<p>Similarly, two bonds may face interest-rate risk but respond differently to interest-rate changes.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the investor must also assess the significance of risk, estimate expected return and determine whether the return adequately compensates for the uncertainty accepted.</p>\n<p>The chapter's decision framework recommends estimating expected returns, assessing risk using historical or projected return variability, comparing the risk-return relationships of alternatives and selecting investments consistent with investor risk preferences.</p>\n<p>Applying that framework to the case, the company should examine the effects of financial leverage, changes in interest rates, inflation, market-wide downturns and foreign exchange movements on expected investment outcomes.</p>\n<p>It should then determine whether those risks justify a higher required return or make alternative investments more suitable.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Risk classification explains what can go wrong, but a sound investment decision must also consider how significantly outcomes may differ from expectations and whether the investor is sufficiently compensated for bearing those risks.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Standard deviation and coefficient of variation (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor is comparing two securities using five years of historical annual returns.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3%</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>35%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For this question, use the sample standard deviation formula with denominator $n-1$.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Calculate the arithmetic mean annual return for each security.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the sample standard deviation of both securities.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Calculate and interpret the coefficient of variation (CV) for both securities.</p>\n<p>(d) [6 marks] Compare the two securities using absolute and relative measures of risk. Critically evaluate whether the results are sufficient to determine the superior investment.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6 · Highest priority for numerical risk assessment</em></p>\n<p>This question is especially useful because it requires you to understand the distinction between absolute risk and relative risk.</p>\n<p>The chapter defines standard deviation as dispersion around the average return, and coefficient of variation as relative dispersion useful for comparing investments with different average returns.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Arithmetic mean returns</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The formula for arithmetic mean return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\bar R=\\frac{\\sum R_t}{n}}$$</div>\n<p>For Security A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_A=\\frac{3+6+9+12+15}{5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\bar R_A=9\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For Security B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_B=\\frac{15+20+25+30+35}{5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\bar R_B=25\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Security B has a substantially higher historical average return.</p>\n<p>However, this alone does not determine whether it is superior.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Sample standard deviation</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The sample standard deviation is calculated as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ s=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum_{t=1}^{n}(R_t-\\bar R)^2}{n-1}} }$$</div>\n<p>The numerator measures the squared deviations of individual returns from their average.</p>\n<p>Dividing by $n-1$ produces sample variance.</p>\n<p>Taking the square root converts variance into standard deviation, expressed in the same return units as the original data.</p>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<p>Average return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_A=9\\%$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Return</th>\n<th>Deviation from 9%</th>\n<th>Squared deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3%</td>\n<td>-6</td>\n<td>36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>-3</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>+3</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>+6</td>\n<td>36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>90</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$s_A=\\sqrt{\\frac{90}{5-1}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{22.5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_A\\approx4.74\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<p>Average return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_B=25\\%$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Return</th>\n<th>Deviation from 25%</th>\n<th>Squared deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>-10</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>-5</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25%</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>+5</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>35%</td>\n<td>+10</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$s_B=\\sqrt{\\frac{250}{5-1}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{62.5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_B\\approx7.91\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Interpretation</p>\n<p>Security B has higher historical standard deviation, indicating that its returns were more dispersed around their mean.</p>\n<p>Therefore, based on the chapter's absolute volatility measure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_B&gt;s_A}$$</div>\n<p>Security B has greater measured absolute risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Coefficient of variation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The coefficient of variation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV=\\frac{s}{\\bar R}}$$</div>\n<p>For Security A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$CV_A=\\frac{4.7434}{9}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV_A\\approx0.527}$$</div>\n<p>For Security B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$CV_B=\\frac{7.9057}{25}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV_B\\approx0.316}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mean return</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample SD</td>\n<td>4.74%</td>\n<td>7.91%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CV</td>\n<td>0.527</td>\n<td>0.316</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Although Security B has greater standard deviation, it has a lower coefficient of variation.</p>\n<p>This means Security B exhibits less historical variability relative to its average return.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Critical evaluation</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Using standard deviation, Security A appears less risky because its historical returns deviate less from its average.</p>\n<p>Using the coefficient of variation, Security B appears more attractive in terms of variability relative to average return.</p>\n<p>This difference arises because Security B's mean return is substantially larger than Security A's.</p>\n<p>Thus, the investment ranking depends on the risk measure being used.</p>\n<p><strong>Comparing the two risk measures:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk measure</th>\n<th>Lower for</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Absolute volatility (SD)</td>\n<td><strong>Security A</strong></td>\n<td>SD = 4.74%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relative dispersion (CV)</td>\n<td><strong>Security B</strong></td>\n<td>CV = 0.316</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>However, selecting the superior investment requires additional consideration.</p>\n<p>First, historical returns do not guarantee future returns. A higher historical mean does not ensure that Security B will continue delivering 25%.</p>\n<p>Second, the sample contains only five observations, so the calculated statistics provide limited evidence about future uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Third, standard deviation treats positive and negative deviations as variability. Consequently, it does not distinguish favorable volatility from unfavorable volatility.</p>\n<p>Finally, the investor's required return and risk preferences must be considered.</p>\n<p>A highly risk-averse investor may prefer Security A's lower absolute volatility.</p>\n<p>Another investor may consider Security B more attractive because of its higher historical mean and lower relative dispersion, but that conclusion depends on whether the historical figures are reasonable estimates for the future.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Security A has lower absolute historical volatility, while Security B has lower relative dispersion. Neither can be declared universally superior without understanding the investor's objectives and the reliability of expected returns.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>Standard deviation and CV answer different questions.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma:\\text{ How much do returns fluctuate?}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV:\\text{ How much variability exists relative to the mean?}}$$</div>\n<p>A lower CV is useful for comparing positive average returns, but it is not a universal proof that an investment is better.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Integrating return, risk and investor preferences (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor is evaluating three potential investments.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Investment A</th>\n<th>Investment B</th>\n<th>Investment C</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected return</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard deviation</td>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assessed risk premium</td>\n<td>3%</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Assume the risk-free rate is 4%.</p>\n<p>The investor is moderately risk-averse and wants to select an investment offering reasonable expected compensation for the risk accepted.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the required return for each investment.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Compare each investment's expected return with its required return and evaluate its acceptability.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Differentiate risk-averse, risk-indifferent and risk-seeking investors, explaining how their preferences affect decisions.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Construct and justify a decision framework for choosing among the three alternatives, including the limitations of the available data.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objectives 3 and 6</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Required returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter expresses required return as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{required}}=R_f+RP}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%$$</div>\n<p>Investment A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_A=4\\%+3\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_A=7\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Investment B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_B=4\\%+7\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_B=11\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Investment C:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_C=4\\%+14\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_C=18\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Required return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>11%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks for each calculation with appropriate substitution.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Evaluating acceptability</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The appropriate initial comparison is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_i)-R_{\\text{required},i}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8\\%-7\\%=+1\\text{ percentage point}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$12\\%-11\\%=+1\\text{ percentage point}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment C:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$16\\%-18\\%=-2\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Expected return compared with required return</strong> (hypothetical Chapter 4 investment alternatives):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">              0%        5%        10%       15%       20%\n              ├─────────┼─────────┼─────────┼─────────┤\n A  expected  ████████████████                           8%\n    required  ██████████████                             7%   → clears the hurdle (+1)\n B  expected  ████████████████████████                  12%\n    required  ██████████████████████                    11%   → clears the hurdle (+1)\n C  expected  ████████████████████████████████          16%\n    required  ████████████████████████████████████      18%   → fails the hurdle (-2)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Investments A and B meet their respective required-return hurdles.</p>\n<p>Investment C fails because its expected return is lower than the return required to compensate for its assessed risk.</p>\n<p>Although C has the highest expected return, it also has the highest standard deviation and a substantially higher required return.</p>\n<p>Therefore, C should not be preferred solely because its expected return is 16%.</p>\n<p>A and B both exceed their required returns by one percentage point.</p>\n<p>However, A has a standard deviation of only 6%, compared with B's 18%.</p>\n<p>This makes A a defensible choice for a moderately risk-averse investor, provided the risk premiums and expected returns were assessed reliably.</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, B might be appropriate for an investor willing to accept substantially higher volatility in pursuit of a higher expected return.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: A and B are acceptable based on their required-return hurdles, while C is not. The final choice between A and B depends on the investor's risk tolerance and confidence in the estimates.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investor risk preferences</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes three attitudes toward risk.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investor type</th>\n<th>Behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-averse</td>\n<td>Requires additional return to accept greater risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-indifferent</td>\n<td>Does not require a change in expected return as compensation for additional risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-seeking</td>\n<td>Is willing to accept lower return in exchange for greater risk, under the chapter's definition</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A risk-averse investor may accept greater risk, but only if adequately compensated.</p>\n<p>This is different from refusing all risky investments.</p>\n<p>A risk-indifferent investor does not increase required return when risk increases.</p>\n<p>A risk-seeking investor, as described by the chapter, is attracted to risk and may accept reduced expected compensation in exchange for greater risk.</p>\n<p>Most conventional investment evaluation is based on risk aversion, where higher required compensation accompanies greater risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Constructing the investment decision process</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter concludes by recommending four broad steps for combining risk and return.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Estimate expected returns:</strong> historical and projected returns, cash flows and valuation.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess investment risk:</strong> risk sources, standard deviation and coefficient of variation.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluate risk-return compensation:</strong> expected return compared with required return.</li>\n<li><strong>Select a suitable investment:</strong> the highest acceptable expected compensation consistent with investor risk preferences.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The investor should first verify the basis for the expected returns and risk premiums.</p>\n<p>The next step is to determine whether each investment's expected return is sufficient for the risk accepted.</p>\n<p>Alternatives that do not meet their required returns should generally be rejected under the chapter's framework.</p>\n<p>For alternatives meeting the hurdle, the investor should compare expected return with measured risk and select the opportunity best aligned with their willingness to tolerate uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Importantly, the provided information does not establish how the assets interact within a diversified portfolio, nor does it guarantee the accuracy of expected returns. A broader portfolio decision would require additional analysis.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investment A is a reasonable preliminary choice for the moderately risk-averse investor because it satisfies its required return while exhibiting substantially lower volatility than B. However, this is a conditional recommendation rather than proof that A will generate the best actual future result.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><span>Simple interest, compound interest and compounding frequency (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor has RM5,000 available for a four-year investment.</p>\n<p>Three financial institutions offer the same nominal annual interest rate of 5%, but use different interest calculation methods.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Institution</th>\n<th>Interest method</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>5% simple interest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>5% compounded annually</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>5% compounded semiannually</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Differentiate simple interest and compound interest. Explain why compounding is economically significant.</p>\n<p>(b) [8 marks] Calculate the future value of RM5,000 after four years for each institution. Compare the results.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Calculate the effective annual interest rate for 5% nominal interest compounded semiannually and monthly.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Critically evaluate whether the investment with the most frequent compounding is necessarily the best investment.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Interest and compounding</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Simple versus compound interest</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Interest represents compensation paid by a borrower for using a lender's money.</p>\n<p>Under simple interest, interest is calculated only on the original principal. Previously earned interest does not generate additional interest.</p>\n<p>Under compound interest, interest is earned on both the original principal and accumulated interest from earlier periods.</p>\n<p>The distinction becomes increasingly important as the investment horizon increases.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Simple Interest}=P(1+rt)}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Compound Interest}=P(1+r)^t}$$</div>\n<p>The first formula produces linear growth, whereas the second produces compounded growth.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing the future values</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>Initial deposit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Annual nominal interest:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=5\\%$$</div>\n<p>Investment horizon:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$t=4\\text{ years}$$</div>\n<p>Institution A - Simple interest</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV_A=5{,}000(1+0.05(4))$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=5{,}000(1.20)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV_A=\\text{RM}6{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The investor earns RM250 interest each year based on the original RM5,000 principal.</p>\n<p>Institution B - Compounded annually</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV_B=5{,}000(1.05)^4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV_B=\\text{RM}6{,}077.53}$$</div>\n<p>The investment earns additional interest because accumulated interest becomes part of the account balance used to calculate subsequent interest.</p>\n<p>Institution C - Compounded semiannually</p>\n<p>Compounding occurs twice each year.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$m=2$$</div>\n<p>Periodic interest rate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r_p=\\frac{0.05}{2}=0.025$$</div>\n<p>Number of compounding periods:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$N=4(2)=8$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV_C=5{,}000(1.025)^8$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV_C=\\text{RM}6{,}092.01}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Institution</th>\n<th>Future value</th>\n<th>Total interest earned</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A - Simple</td>\n<td>RM6,000.00</td>\n<td>RM1,000.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B - Annual compounding</td>\n<td>RM6,077.53</td>\n<td>RM1,077.53</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C - Semiannual compounding</td>\n<td>RM6,092.01</td>\n<td>RM1,092.01</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Under the stated assumptions, Institution C produces the highest future value.</p>\n<p>The difference between B and C is only RM14.48. This demonstrates that more frequent compounding increases wealth, but the additional benefit may be relatively small.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Effective annual rate</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The effective annual rate (EAR) represents the actual annual compound return implied by the stated nominal interest rate and its compounding frequency.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{EAR=\\left(1+\\frac{r}{m}\\right)^m-1}$$</div>\n<p>For semiannual compounding:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EAR=\\left(1+\\frac{0.05}{2}\\right)^2-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{EAR=5.0625\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For monthly compounding:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EAR=\\left(1+\\frac{0.05}{12}\\right)^{12}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{EAR\\approx5.1162\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The appendix's Table 4A.3 makes this same comparison using a 5% stated rate. It shows an increase from 5.000% with annual compounding toward approximately 5.127% with continuous compounding.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating the best investment</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Institution C provides the highest future value among the three specified choices, assuming identical risk, fees, liquidity, taxes and other terms.</p>\n<p>However, the investment offering the highest compounding frequency is not necessarily the best overall.</p>\n<p>For example, an investment with monthly compounding might impose withdrawal restrictions or additional fees that outweigh the relatively small interest benefit.</p>\n<p>Similarly, an investor who needs funds before maturity may place greater value on liquidity than on a marginally higher effective interest rate.</p>\n<p>Credit risk and the treatment of interest income can also affect the investment's attractiveness.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: More frequent compounding increases the effective return when the nominal annual rate is fixed and positive, but investment suitability must be evaluated using the complete terms of the investment rather than compounding frequency alone.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><span>Future value, present value and financial planning (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor is evaluating several financial goals.</p>\n<p>The available annual rate of return is assumed to be 8%, compounded annually.</p>\n<p>The investor wants to determine the value of money at different dates and whether current savings can finance a future financial obligation.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Explain the time value of money and distinguish future value from present value.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the future value of RM1,000 invested for two years and the present value of RM1,000 receivable in seven years.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] An investor must make a RM20,000 payment four years from today. Calculate the amount required today to finance this payment at 8%. Determine whether investing RM15,000 today would be sufficient.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate the assumptions underlying the calculations and explain how a spreadsheet can support time-value-of-money analysis.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Lump-sum valuation and financial planning</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Time value of money</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The time value of money recognises that the value of money depends on when it is received.</p>\n<p>Money received earlier can be invested to generate returns, making it more valuable than an identical nominal amount received later under positive interest rates.</p>\n<p>Future value determines how much money invested today will grow to at a specified future date.</p>\n<p>Present value determines what a future cash flow is worth today, given an appropriate discount rate.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Future Value = Compounding Forward}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Present Value = Discounting Backward}}$$</div>\n<p>These calculations allow investors to compare financial amounts occurring at different dates.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Calculating FV and PV</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Future value of RM1,000 after two years</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=PV(1+r)^n$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=1{,}000(1.08)^2$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}1{,}166.40}$$</div>\n<p>This means RM1,000 invested today will grow to RM1,166.40 after two years at 8% compounded annually.</p>\n<p>Present value of RM1,000 receivable in seven years</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\frac{FV}{(1+r)^n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\frac{1{,}000}{(1.08)^7}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV=\\text{RM}583.49}$$</div>\n<p>This means RM583.49 invested today at 8% compounded annually would grow to approximately RM1,000 after seven years.</p>\n<p><strong>The time-value timeline</strong> (illustrative, for the two-year FV calculation):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">   t0                   t1                   t2\n   ├────────────────────┼────────────────────┤\n RM1,000 ──x1.08──► RM1,080 ──x1.08──► RM1,166.40     compounding: move FORWARD\n\n RM583.49 ◄────────── ÷ (1.08)^7 ─────────── RM1,000 at t7   discounting: move BACKWARD\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>(c) Financing a RM20,000 future payment</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investor requires:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=\\text{RM}20{,}000$$</div>\n<p>after:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=4\\text{ years}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Calculate the required initial deposit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\frac{20{,}000}{(1.08)^4}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV=\\text{RM}14{,}700.60}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, approximately RM14,700.60 must be invested today to accumulate RM20,000 four years later at the assumed rate.</p>\n<p>The investor currently has RM15,000.</p>\n<p>Calculate its future value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=15{,}000(1.08)^4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}20{,}407.33}$$</div>\n<p>The amount exceeds the required payment by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$20{,}407.33-20{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}407.33}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, RM15,000 would be sufficient under the assumed 8% annual compound return.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation of assumptions and spreadsheet analysis</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The calculation assumes the 8% return can be earned consistently throughout the four-year period.</p>\n<p>It also assumes that no withdrawals reduce the invested principal and that relevant investment costs, taxes and inflation effects do not invalidate the financial objective.</p>\n<p>If the actual return is lower than 8%, the investor may fail to accumulate RM20,000.</p>\n<p>The projected surplus of RM407.33 is modest, so it should not be treated as substantial protection against uncertainty.</p>\n<p>The appendix explains that spreadsheet routines allow investors to change inputs and automatically recalculate financial values.</p>\n<p>For example, Excel can calculate:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Excel formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Required PV</td>\n<td><code>=PV(8%,4,0,-20000)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FV of RM15,000</td>\n<td><code>=FV(8%,4,0,-15000)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The negative numbers indicate the cash amounts paid or deposited by the investor, consistent with the financial-function sign convention.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Time-value calculations are essential for determining the capital required today to meet future obligations, but their usefulness depends on realistic estimates of investment returns and cash-flow timing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><span>Future value of an ordinary annuity (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A university graduate wishes to accumulate long-term savings.</p>\n<p>The graduate plans to deposit RM1,000 at the end of each year for eight years into an account earning 8% interest compounded annually.</p>\n<p>A financial advisor suggests that the investor should understand how the timing of deposits affects future wealth.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Define an annuity and an ordinary annuity. Explain why payment timing matters.</p>\n<p>(b) [8 marks] Calculate the future value of the eight-year ordinary annuity.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Suppose the investor could instead deposit RM8,000 immediately and leave it invested for eight years at the same rate. Calculate its future value.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate the differences between these saving strategies, including the practical limitations of comparing them based only on their ending balances.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Annuities and investment accumulation</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Annuity and ordinary annuity</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>An annuity is a series of equal cash flows occurring at equally spaced intervals.</p>\n<p>An ordinary annuity is a particular type of annuity in which each payment occurs at the end of the period.</p>\n<p>For example, depositing RM1,000 at the end of every year for eight years represents an ordinary annuity.</p>\n<p>The timing is important because different deposits earn interest for different lengths of time.</p>\n<p>The first deposit accumulates interest for seven years until the end of Year 8, whereas the final deposit earns no additional interest before the valuation date at the end of Year 8.</p>\n<p>Therefore, annuity valuation must account for the timing of each payment rather than simply multiplying the periodic deposit by the number of payments.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Future value of the annuity</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>Annual payment:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PMT=\\text{RM}1{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Annual interest:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Number of payments:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=8$$</div>\n<p>The future value of an ordinary annuity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ FV_{\\text{annuity}} = PMT\\left[\\frac{(1+r)^n-1}{r}\\right] }$$</div>\n<p>Substitute the data:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=1{,}000\\left[\\frac{(1.08)^8-1}{0.08}\\right]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=1{,}000(10.6366276)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}10{,}636.63}$$</div>\n<p>The total amount deposited is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8(\\text{RM}1{,}000)=\\text{RM}8{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, interest earned is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10{,}636.63-8{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}636.63}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Ordinary annuity accumulation:</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> total contributions   RM8,000.00  ████████████████████████\n accumulated interest  RM2,636.63  ████████\n                                   ────────────────────────────────\n ending value         RM10,636.63  ████████████████████████████████\n</code></pre>\n<p>The first deposit compounds for seven years and the eighth deposit occurs at the valuation date.</p>\n<p>The appendix uses this same eight-year, 8% ordinary-annuity example and obtains a future value of 10,636.63.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investing RM8,000 immediately</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>If RM8,000 is invested at the beginning of the eight-year period, the entire amount earns interest for eight years.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=8{,}000(1.08)^8$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}14{,}807.44}$$</div>\n<p>Compare this with the ordinary annuity:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment strategy</th>\n<th>Future value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM1,000 at each year-end for eight years</td>\n<td>RM10,636.63</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM8,000 deposited immediately</td>\n<td>RM14,807.44</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Difference</td>\n<td>RM4,170.81</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lump-sum investment produces greater future wealth because all RM8,000 earns compound interest for the full eight years.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation of the strategies</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Although the lump-sum investment generates a higher future value, it should not automatically be declared the superior investment strategy.</p>\n<p>The two strategies commit capital at different times.</p>\n<p>The lump-sum investor must provide the entire RM8,000 immediately, while the annuity investor contributes funds gradually.</p>\n<p>In fact, the present value of the eight RM1,000 year-end contributions, discounted at 8%, is approximately RM5,746.64.</p>\n<p>Thus, the two strategies do not require economically equivalent capital commitments at the beginning.</p>\n<p>A lump-sum strategy may benefit an investor who already has sufficient savings available.</p>\n<p>An ordinary-annuity saving plan may be more practical for an individual who accumulates investment capital through regular employment income.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Earlier investment generally produces more compound interest when the return is positive, but the appropriate saving strategy depends on capital availability, cash-flow requirements and the investor's financial circumstances.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><span>Present value of annuities and mixed streams (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor is comparing two investment opportunities. Each requires an initial investment of RM195 and provides cash flows over five years.</p>\n<p>The cash flows correspond to the structures used in Appendix 4A, Table 4A.4.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>End of year</th>\n<th>Investment A: Mixed stream</th>\n<th>Investment B: Ordinary annuity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM30</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM40</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>RM70</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal total</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Assume both investments have comparable risk and the investor requires an 8% annual return.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Distinguish a mixed cash-flow stream from an ordinary annuity, explaining why this distinction matters for valuation.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the present value of Investment A's cash flows.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Calculate the present value of Investment B's cash flows. Compare the present values and evaluate both investments using the satisfactory-investment rule from Chapter 4.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Critically evaluate why two investments with identical nominal total cash flows can have different economic values.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Cash-flow valuation and investment selection</em></p>\n<p>This question illustrates a particularly important financial principle:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Two investments can distribute exactly the same total amount of cash yet have different present values because the timing of those payments differs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>(a) Mixed streams versus ordinary annuities</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>A mixed cash-flow stream consists of payments that do not follow a uniform pattern.</p>\n<p>For example, an investment paying RM30, RM40, RM50, RM60 and RM70 over five years produces an unequal series of payments.</p>\n<p>An ordinary annuity consists of equal payments made at the end of regular periods.</p>\n<p>For example, receiving RM50 at the end of every year for five years represents an ordinary annuity.</p>\n<p>Both streams can be valued by discounting every cash flow individually.</p>\n<p>However, the annuity's uniform structure permits a simplified valuation formula.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Present value of Investment A</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Investment A pays:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Cash flow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>RM70</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Required return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>For an uneven stream:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} PV_A={}&amp;\\frac{30}{1.08} +\\frac{40}{1.08^2} +\\frac{50}{1.08^3}\\\\ &amp;+\\frac{60}{1.08^4} +\\frac{70}{1.08^5} \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p>Each cash flow contributes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Cash flow</th>\n<th>Present value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM30</td>\n<td>RM27.78</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM40</td>\n<td>RM34.29</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n<td>RM39.69</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td>RM44.10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>RM70</td>\n<td>RM47.64</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n<td>RM193.51</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Thus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_A\\approx \\text{RM}193.51}$$</div>\n<p>This reproduces the present-value result in Appendix 4A, Table 4A.5, using the source's five-year mixed cash-flow example.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Present value of Investment B and investment decision</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Investment B pays RM50 at the end of every year for five years.</p>\n<p>The present value of an ordinary annuity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ PV_{\\text{annuity}} = PMT\\left[\\frac{1-(1+r)^{-n}}{r}\\right] }$$</div>\n<p>Substitute:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PMT=\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=5$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_B = 50\\left[\\frac{1-(1.08)^{-5}}{0.08}\\right]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_B=\\text{RM}199.64}$$</div>\n<p>Now compare the two investments.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Investment A</th>\n<th>Investment B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal cash inflows</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present value of inflows</td>\n<td>RM193.51</td>\n<td>RM199.64</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial cost</td>\n<td>RM195.00</td>\n<td>RM195.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NPV</td>\n<td>-RM1.49</td>\n<td>+RM4.64</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Chapter 4 satisfactory-investment rule is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV(\\text{Benefits})\\ge PV(\\text{Costs})}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NPV_A=193.51-195$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{NPV_A=-\\text{RM}1.49}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NPV_B=199.64-195$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{NPV_B=+\\text{RM}4.64}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment</th>\n<th>NPV</th>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Decision</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td><strong>-RM1.49</strong></td>\n<td>NPV below zero</td>\n<td>Reject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td><strong>+RM4.64</strong></td>\n<td>NPV above zero</td>\n<td>Accept</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Based on the assumed 8% required return and equal initial cost, Investment A fails the satisfactory-investment test while Investment B satisfies it.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Investment B is the preferred alternative under the stated assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Why identical nominal payments have different values</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The two investments provide identical total nominal cash flows of RM250.</p>\n<p>However, their payment schedules differ.</p>\n<p>Investment A provides relatively small payments in the earlier years and larger payments later.</p>\n<p>Investment B provides consistent payments of RM50 from the first year onward.</p>\n<p>Earlier cash flows are more valuable because investors can receive and potentially reinvest them sooner.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the cash-flow timing produces a higher present value for Investment B.</p>\n<p>This difference can be evaluated mathematically through discounting.</p>\n<p>The result also depends on the chosen discount rate.</p>\n<p>For example, increasing the required return from 8% to 12% would reduce the present values of both investments, making neither satisfactory at an initial RM195 cost.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates that present-value decisions depend on both expected cash flows and the return required by the investor.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investment B provides a higher present value because more of its cash flows arrive earlier. Investment evaluation must therefore consider the timing of benefits, not merely their nominal total.</p></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"l4s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">16 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - CHAPTER 4: RETURN AND RISK</li><li data-full=\"1. How to write a high-scoring Chapter 4 answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s2\" title=\"1. How to write a high-scoring Chapter 4 answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>How to write a high-scoring Chapter 4 answer</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s3\" title=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Full mock subjective examination</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s4\" title=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s5\" title=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Additional short-answer questions for revision</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Chapter 4 formula sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s6\" title=\"5. Chapter 4 formula sheet\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Chapter 4 formula sheet</button></li><li data-full=\"6. How to allocate marks in subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s7\" title=\"6. How to allocate marks in subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>How to allocate marks in subjective questions</button></li><li data-full=\"7. What I would prioritize for Chapter 4\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s8\" title=\"7. What I would prioritize for Chapter 4\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>What I would prioritize for Chapter 4</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Final higher-order examination exercise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s9\" title=\"8. Final higher-order examination exercise\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Final higher-order examination exercise</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - APPENDIX 4A: TIME VALUE OF MONEY</li><li data-full=\"Appendix 4A subjective overview\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s11\" title=\"Appendix 4A subjective overview\">Appendix 4A subjective overview</button></li><li data-full=\"1. Additional subjective examination - Appendix 4A\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s12\" title=\"1. Additional subjective examination - Appendix 4A\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Appendix 4A mock questions 7-10</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Appendix 4A - Full model answers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s13\" title=\"2. Appendix 4A - Full model answers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Appendix 4A model answers</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Appendix 4A formula sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s14\" title=\"3. Appendix 4A formula sheet\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Appendix 4A formula sheet</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Choosing the appropriate formula in an exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s15\" title=\"4. Choosing the appropriate formula in an exam\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Choosing the appropriate formula in an exam</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Additional Appendix 4A short-answer practice\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s16\" title=\"5. Additional Appendix 4A short-answer practice\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Additional Appendix 4A short-answer practice</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Combined Chapter 4 + 4A revision priorities\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s17\" title=\"6. Combined Chapter 4 + 4A revision priorities\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Combined Chapter 4 + 4A revision priorities</button></li><li data-full=\"7. The final integrated principle\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s18\" title=\"7. The final integrated principle\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>The final integrated principle</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 4 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>16 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 4 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Return and Risk</p>\n<p>Chapter 4 is arguably one of the most important chapters for technical subjective questions because it requires you to combine financial calculations with investment judgment.</p>\n<p>It is not enough to calculate that Investment A earns 15% while Investment B earns 10%. You must determine whether the additional return justifies the additional risk, whether the returns are being measured over comparable periods, and whether the investment meets the investor's required return.</p>\n<p>This preparation covers all six learning objectives in your uploaded Chapter 4 slides: return and its components, time value of money, required return and HPR, yield and growth rates, sources of risk, and quantitative risk assessment.</p>\n<p>Part A focuses on Chapter 4 itself. Time value of money is included where Chapter 4 uses it for valuation and IRR; the detailed annuity and compounding exercises are in Part B (Appendix 4A).</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4s-d1\">PART A - CHAPTER 4: RETURN AND RISK</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. How to write a high-scoring Chapter 4 answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For numerical and investment-evaluation questions, follow this sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Identify the financial principle:</strong> Determine whether the problem concerns total return, HPR, IRR, required return, real return or investment risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Calculate accurately:</strong> Present the formula, substitute the data, show your calculation and state the answer with appropriate units.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Analyse what the result means:</strong> Explain the economic relationship. For example, distinguish higher return from adequate risk compensation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Evaluate and recommend:</strong> Compare alternatives using the investor's required return and risk preference. Identify assumptions before reaching a conclusion.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Example: Weak versus strong answer</h4>\n<p>Question: Evaluate whether a 15% expected return makes an investment attractive.</p>\n<p><strong>Weak answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A 15% return is attractive because it is a high return and would allow investors to earn more profit.</p>\n<p><strong>Stronger answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An investment's attractiveness cannot be determined solely from its expected return. Under the risk-return trade-off, the investor must compare the expected return with the required return, which incorporates the risk-free rate and an investment-specific risk premium. For example, if the required return is 18%, an expected return of 15% provides insufficient compensation, despite appearing high in absolute terms. Conversely, if the required return is 10%, the same investment may be financially acceptable. However, this conclusion depends on the reliability of the expected cash flows, the assessment of risk and the investor's ability to bear unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, 15% should be evaluated relative to its risk rather than treated as universally attractive.</p>\n<p>The stronger answer earns marks for the principle, financial mechanism, application, conditional evaluation and justified conclusion.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Full mock subjective examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>Questions:</strong> 6 · <strong>Total marks:</strong> 120 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 120 min</p>\n<p>Six 20-mark questions using original scenarios and selected textbook-style figures. The paper emphasizes application, analysis and evaluation. Complete the questions independently before reading the worked answers.</p>\n<h4>Question 1 - Investment returns and performance evaluation (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor is evaluating two securities over a one-year holding period.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Information</th>\n<th>Investment A</th>\n<th>Investment B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beginning share price</td>\n<td>RM100</td>\n<td>RM80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ending share price</td>\n<td>RM112</td>\n<td>RM75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Income received</td>\n<td>RM3</td>\n<td>RM9</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The investor claims that Investment B is superior because it provides three times as much cash income.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the capital gain or loss, total return in RM and holding-period return (HPR) for both investments.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Distinguish income return, capital gain or loss, realized return and paper return.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Critically evaluate the investor's claim and explain the limitations of relying solely on historical investment returns.</p>\n<p>(d) [5 marks] Analyse how internal characteristics and external economic forces may affect future returns.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 2 - Time value of money and required return (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A company offers an investment opportunity requiring RM12,000 immediately.</p>\n<p>The investment is expected to generate the following end-of-year cash flows:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Expected cash flow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM4,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM5,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM6,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An investor estimates a real required return component of 3%, an expected inflation premium of 2% and an investment-specific risk premium of 5%.</p>\n<p>(a) [5 marks] Explain the concept of time value of money and why future investment benefits must be discounted.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate the risk-free rate and required return using the chapter's model.</p>\n<p>(c) [7 marks] Calculate the present value of the expected benefits and determine whether the investment satisfies the chapter's satisfactory-investment rule.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate two important assumptions that could change the investment decision.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 3 - HPR, IRR and dividend growth (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investment analyst reviews four opportunities.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Investment A: A stock purchased for RM50 is worth RM57 one year later and has paid RM3 in dividends.</li>\n<li>Investment B: An asset costing RM1,000 is expected to be worth RM1,400 after five years, with no interim income.</li>\n<li>Investment C: A company's dividend increased from RM0.92 to RM1.85 over nine years.</li>\n<li>Investment D: An investment costs RM1,000 today and is expected to pay RM600 at the end of Year 1 and RM600 at the end of Year 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Assume Investment D has a required return of 12%.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Calculate and interpret Investment A's HPR.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate Investment B's compound annual yield and explain why its five-year total return should not be confused with an annual return.</p>\n<p>(c) [3 marks] Calculate Investment C's compound annual dividend growth rate.</p>\n<p>(d) [6 marks] Calculate Investment D's IRR and evaluate its acceptability under the chapter's decision rule.</p>\n<p>(e) [3 marks] Explain the interest-on-interest and reinvestment-rate assumption discussed in the chapter.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 4 - Identifying and evaluating investment risks (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A diversified investment company is facing the following developments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One operating subsidiary is losing market share because of poor management decisions.</li>\n<li>Another subsidiary relies heavily on debt and is struggling to meet interest obligations.</li>\n<li>Inflation increases while the company holds fixed-income securities.</li>\n<li>Market interest rates rise, reducing the value of existing fixed-rate bonds.</li>\n<li>A property must be sold quickly at a substantial discount.</li>\n<li>An unfavorable change in taxation reduces the after-tax benefit of an investment.</li>\n<li>A major hurricane unexpectedly damages insured properties.</li>\n<li>A broad economic downturn causes many securities to decline.</li>\n<li>Foreign-currency movements reduce the domestic value of international investments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(a) [9 marks] Identify and explain the nine sources of risk illustrated in the scenario.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Differentiate business risk from financial risk, and event risk from market risk.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Evaluate why identifying risk categories is necessary but insufficient for selecting a suitable investment. Recommend how the company should incorporate these risks into its investment assessment.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Standard deviation and coefficient of variation (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor is comparing two securities using five years of historical annual returns.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3%</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>35%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For this question, use the sample standard deviation formula with denominator $n-1$.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Calculate the arithmetic mean annual return for each security.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the sample standard deviation of both securities.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Calculate and interpret the coefficient of variation (CV) for both securities.</p>\n<p>(d) [6 marks] Compare the two securities using absolute and relative measures of risk. Critically evaluate whether the results are sufficient to determine the superior investment.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Integrating return, risk and investor preferences (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor is evaluating three potential investments.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Investment A</th>\n<th>Investment B</th>\n<th>Investment C</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected return</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard deviation</td>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assessed risk premium</td>\n<td>3%</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Assume the risk-free rate is 4%.</p>\n<p>The investor is moderately risk-averse and wants to select an investment offering reasonable expected compensation for the risk accepted.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the required return for each investment.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Compare each investment's expected return with its required return and evaluate its acceptability.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Differentiate risk-averse, risk-indifferent and risk-seeking investors, explaining how their preferences affect decisions.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Construct and justify a decision framework for choosing among the three alternatives, including the limitations of the available data.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Model answers and marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These answers are written to demonstrate how to earn marks for calculations, economic reasoning and critical evaluation. The suggested mark allocations are illustrative.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 1 - Investment returns and performance evaluation</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 1</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Capital gain, total return and HPR</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines total return as the sum of income and capital gain or loss over a specified period.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Capital Gain/Loss}=P_1-P_0$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Total Return}=Income+(P_1-P_0)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR=\\frac{Income+(P_1-P_0)}{P_0}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Investment A</strong></p>\n<p>Capital gain:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}112-\\text{RM}100=\\text{RM}12$$</div>\n<p>Total return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}3+\\text{RM}12=\\boxed{\\text{RM}15}$$</div>\n<p>Holding-period return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$HPR_A=\\frac{3+12}{100}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR_A=15\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Investment B</strong></p>\n<p>Capital loss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}75-\\text{RM}80=-\\text{RM}5$$</div>\n<p>Total return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}9-\\text{RM}5=\\boxed{\\text{RM}4}$$</div>\n<p>Holding-period return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$HPR_B=\\frac{9-5}{80}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR_B=5\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>A</th>\n<th>B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Income</td>\n<td>RM3</td>\n<td>RM9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital gain/loss</td>\n<td>+RM12</td>\n<td>-RM5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total return</td>\n<td>RM15</td>\n<td>RM4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HPR</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 3 marks per investment for the capital gain/loss, total return and HPR.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Return components and realization</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Income return represents cash or near-cash received from owning an investment, such as dividends and interest.</p>\n<p>Capital gain or loss represents the difference between the investment's selling proceeds and original purchase price. When shares are still held, changes in market value can be included in a holding-period valuation, but the gains or losses have not been realized through sale.</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes realized returns from paper returns.</p>\n<p>A realized return has been actually received by the investor, while a paper return reflects an increase or decrease in investment value that has not yet been realized through disposal.</p>\n<p>For example, if Investment A has not been sold, the RM12 increase in its share value represents an unrealized capital gain, while its RM3 dividend has already been received.</p>\n<p>If the investor sells the shares at RM112, the capital gain becomes realized.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluation of Investment B</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The claim that Investment B is superior because it provides more income is incomplete.</p>\n<p>Investment B produces RM9 of income compared with RM3 for Investment A. However, it simultaneously suffers a RM5 capital loss.</p>\n<p>Consequently, B's total return is only RM4, equivalent to 5% of its initial value.</p>\n<p>Investment A generates a higher total return of 15% because its capital appreciation more than compensates for its lower dividend income.</p>\n<p>Therefore, A performed better over the historical period according to HPR.</p>\n<p>However, this does not prove that A will be the superior investment in the future.</p>\n<p>The chapter explicitly states that historical returns provide a basis for future expectations but do not guarantee future performance.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the data do not indicate the uncertainty surrounding future returns.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investment A delivered stronger historical total-return performance, but the investor should assess its expected return, risks and required return before making a future investment decision.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Internal and external factors</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies both internal characteristics and external forces affecting investment returns.</p>\n<p>Internal characteristics include the investment type, issuer management and the issuer's financing arrangements.</p>\n<p>For example, poor management decisions may reduce profitability, while excessive borrowing can create additional financial obligations.</p>\n<p>External forces include political, business and economic conditions, inflation and deflation.</p>\n<p>For example, a recession may weaken demand and corporate earnings, while inflation can reduce the purchasing power of investment income.</p>\n<p>These factors may change future investment values even when historical performance was strong.</p>\n<p>Therefore, past returns should be analysed alongside company characteristics and broader economic conditions.</p>\n<p>Why this earns high marks: The answer distinguishes income from total return, demonstrates the calculations, evaluates the historical comparison and recognizes the uncertainty surrounding future performance.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 2 - Time value of money and required return</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objectives 2 and 3 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Time value of money</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The time value of money states that money received today is generally worth more than the same nominal amount received in the future, provided money can earn a positive return.</p>\n<p>This occurs because funds received earlier can be invested and generate additional income.</p>\n<p>Consequently, investment benefits received at different dates cannot be compared directly.</p>\n<p>To evaluate whether an investment is satisfactory, the investor must convert future benefits into their present values using an appropriate discount rate.</p>\n<p>The chapter defines a satisfactory investment as one where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ PV(\\text{Benefits})\\ge PV(\\text{Costs}) }$$</div>\n<p>This rule ensures that the investment's benefits adequately compensate the investor for committing funds over time.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Risk-free and required returns</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter uses the additive relationship:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=R_{\\text{real}}+IP$$</div>\n<p>where $IP$ is the expected inflation premium.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=3\\%+2\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_f=5\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The required return also incorporates an investment-specific risk premium:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{required}}=R_f+RP$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=5\\%+5\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{required}}=10\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The risk-free rate compensates the investor for the real return and expected inflation components under the textbook's model.</p>\n<p>The additional risk premium compensates the investor for the risks associated with the investment and its issuer.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Present value and investment acceptability</strong> [7 marks]</p>\n<p>The present value of the benefits is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$$</div>\n<p>Using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>Year 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_1=\\frac{4{,}000}{1.10}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}3{,}636.36$$</div>\n<p>Year 2:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_2=\\frac{5{,}000}{1.10^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}4{,}132.23$$</div>\n<p>Year 3:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_3=\\frac{6{,}000}{1.10^3}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}4{,}507.89$$</div>\n<p>Total present value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{benefits}} = 3{,}636.36+4{,}132.23+4{,}507.89$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{benefits}}\\approx \\text{RM}12{,}276.48}$$</div>\n<p>Initial cost:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{cost}}=\\text{RM}12{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The difference is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NPV=12{,}276.48-12{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{NPV=+\\text{RM}276.48}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>PV of expected benefits</th>\n<th>Investment cost</th>\n<th>NPV</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM12,276.48</td>\n<td>RM12,000</td>\n<td><strong>+RM276.48</strong> (positive: exceeds the required compensation)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV(\\text{Benefits})&gt;PV(\\text{Cost})$$</div>\n<p>the investment is satisfactory under the chapter's present-value rule and the stated assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating assumptions</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>First, the projected cash flows may be uncertain.</p>\n<p>If the company fails to generate the expected RM4,000, RM5,000 and RM6,000 payments, the actual investment benefits could fall below the estimated present value.</p>\n<p>Second, the 10% required return depends on the assessed risk premium and expected inflation.</p>\n<p>If the investment proves riskier than originally estimated, the investor may require a higher return.</p>\n<p>Increasing the discount rate would reduce the present value of the expected future benefits and could reverse the decision.</p>\n<p>The positive NPV is also relatively small compared with the initial RM12,000 cost, so the investment has limited room for adverse changes in assumptions.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The investment is acceptable using the estimated 10% required return, but management should test the reliability of its cash-flow forecast and risk assessment before approval.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>A positive NPV is a conditional valuation conclusion. It does not guarantee that the future cash flows will actually occur.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 3 - HPR, IRR and dividend growth</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Investment A: HPR</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The beginning share price is RM50, ending value is RM57 and dividend income is RM3.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$HPR=\\frac{3+(57-50)}{50}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac{10}{50}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{HPR=20\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The investment generated a total return of RM10 per share, comprising RM3 income and RM7 capital appreciation.</p>\n<p>This represents 20% of its initial value over the one-year holding period.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Investment B: Compound annual yield</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The asset costs RM1,000 and is expected to be worth RM1,400 after five years.</p>\n<p>The cumulative return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{1{,}400-1{,}000}{1{,}000}=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>However, the 40% return occurs over five years.</p>\n<p>To determine its compound annual yield:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=PV(1+r)^n$$</div>\n<p>Rearranging:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=\\left(\\frac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n}-1$$</div>\n<p>Substituting:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=\\left(\\frac{1{,}400}{1{,}000}\\right)^{1/5}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{r\\approx6.96\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The investment earns a compound annual return of approximately 6.96%, not 40% annually.</p>\n<p>The distinction matters because HPR can mislead investors when comparing investments held for different lengths of time.</p>\n<p>The chapter explicitly identifies this limitation and recommends yield or IRR for longer holding periods.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investment C: Dividend growth</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>Beginning dividend:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D_0=\\text{RM}0.92$$</div>\n<p>Ending dividend:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D_n=\\text{RM}1.85$$</div>\n<p>Number of years:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=9$$</div>\n<p>The compound annual dividend growth rate is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$g=\\left(\\frac{D_n}{D_0}\\right)^{1/n}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$g=\\left(\\frac{1.85}{0.92}\\right)^{1/9}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{g\\approx8.07\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This indicates that the dividend increased at an equivalent compound annual rate of approximately 8.07% over the historical period.</p>\n<p>It does not guarantee that future dividends will grow at the same rate.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Investment D: IRR and acceptability</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investment requires RM1,000 today and produces RM600 at the end of both Years 1 and 2.</p>\n<p>IRR is the discount rate that equates the present value of the future cash-flow stream with the investment's cost.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1{,}000=\\frac{600}{(1+IRR)}+\\frac{600}{(1+IRR)^2}$$</div>\n<p>Solving gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{IRR\\approx13.07\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The required return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{required}}=12\\%$$</div>\n<p>Compare:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IRR=13.07\\%&gt;12\\%$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, the investment exceeds the required return by approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$13.07\\%-12\\%=1.07\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<p>Under the chapter's decision rule:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{IRR\\ge R_{\\text{required}}\\Rightarrow\\text{Acceptable}}$$</div>\n<p>Investment D is acceptable under the stated cash-flow and discount-rate assumptions.</p>\n<p>However, this result does not guarantee that the actual investment return will equal the estimated IRR.</p>\n<p><strong>(e) Interest-on-interest and reinvestment</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter explains that interest on interest occurs when investment income received during the holding period is reinvested and earns additional income.</p>\n<p>The reinvestment rate is the rate earned on the interest or other income received from an investment.</p>\n<p>The chapter associates its fully compounded interpretation of yield with the assumption that interim income is reinvested at the same rate as the original investment's IRR.</p>\n<p>If interim income can only be reinvested at a lower rate, the investor may accumulate less terminal wealth than that assumption suggests.</p>\n<p>A technical distinction is important: the IRR equation itself calculates a discount rate equating investment cost and cash flows. Reinvesting distributions at the IRR becomes relevant when using that rate to describe or project a fully compounded terminal investment outcome.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: HPR, IRR and dividend growth measure different things. HPR measures total return over a holding period, IRR accounts for the timing of cash flows, and dividend growth measures the compound rate of change in a stream of income.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 4 - Sources of investment risk</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Identifying the nine sources of risk</strong> [9 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies nine major sources of risk. Each concerns a different mechanism through which an investment's return or value can be adversely affected.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Company loses market share due to poor management</td>\n<td>Business risk</td>\n<td>Uncertainty surrounding business earnings and the ability to generate returns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Highly indebted company struggles to meet interest obligations</td>\n<td>Financial risk</td>\n<td>Uncertainty arising from the firm's debt and equity financing structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inflation increases while fixed nominal income remains unchanged</td>\n<td>Purchasing-power risk</td>\n<td>Changes in price levels reduce the real value of investment returns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rising interest rates reduce existing bond prices</td>\n<td>Interest-rate risk</td>\n<td>Changes in market interest rates adversely affect security values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Property must be sold at a substantial discount</td>\n<td>Liquidity risk</td>\n<td>Inability to convert an investment conveniently into cash at a reasonable price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tax-law changes reduce investment benefits</td>\n<td>Tax risk</td>\n<td>Unfavorable tax changes reduce after-tax returns and potentially market values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A hurricane damages insured properties</td>\n<td>Event risk</td>\n<td>An unexpected event produces a significant and unusually immediate financial effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broad economic deterioration causes market declines</td>\n<td>Market risk</td>\n<td>Market-wide factors affect investment returns independently of a particular issuer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Foreign-currency depreciation reduces domestic investment value</td>\n<td>Currency exchange risk</td>\n<td>Exchange-rate changes alter returns when foreign investments are valued in the domestic currency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 1 mark per correctly identified and explained risk category.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Differentiating related risks</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>Business risk versus financial risk</p>\n<p>Business risk concerns uncertainty surrounding a company's operations and earnings.</p>\n<p>For example, ineffective management or declining market share may reduce revenue and profitability.</p>\n<p>Financial risk concerns uncertainty arising from the company's capital structure, particularly the proportion of debt financing.</p>\n<p>A company may operate profitably but remain financially vulnerable because of large debt obligations.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Business Risk}=\\text{Operating/Earnings Uncertainty}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Financial Risk}=\\text{Financing-Related Uncertainty}}$$</div>\n<p>These risks are related but not identical. Poor operating performance can make debt obligations harder to service, causing both risks to interact.</p>\n<p>Event risk versus market risk</p>\n<p>Event risk arises from unexpected events producing significant and unusually immediate effects on investments.</p>\n<p>A hurricane that causes substantial insurance claims is an example given in the chapter.</p>\n<p>Market risk arises from broader factors, including political upheaval and changing economic conditions, which can affect securities across the market.</p>\n<p>A broad market decline may affect numerous firms simultaneously, whereas an event may have a more direct impact on a particular issuer.</p>\n<p>However, a severe event can also have wider market consequences. These classifications help identify risk mechanisms; they do not mean real-world events can affect only one category.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluation and recommended assessment</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Identifying risk categories is necessary because investors must understand what could cause an investment to perform differently from expectations.</p>\n<p>However, identification alone is insufficient to determine whether an investment is suitable.</p>\n<p>Two investments may both face business risk, yet differ substantially in the uncertainty of their earnings.</p>\n<p>Similarly, two bonds may face interest-rate risk but respond differently to interest-rate changes.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the investor must also assess the significance of risk, estimate expected return and determine whether the return adequately compensates for the uncertainty accepted.</p>\n<p>The chapter's decision framework recommends estimating expected returns, assessing risk using historical or projected return variability, comparing the risk-return relationships of alternatives and selecting investments consistent with investor risk preferences.</p>\n<p>Applying that framework to the case, the company should examine the effects of financial leverage, changes in interest rates, inflation, market-wide downturns and foreign exchange movements on expected investment outcomes.</p>\n<p>It should then determine whether those risks justify a higher required return or make alternative investments more suitable.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Risk classification explains what can go wrong, but a sound investment decision must also consider how significantly outcomes may differ from expectations and whether the investor is sufficiently compensated for bearing those risks.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 5 - Standard deviation and coefficient of variation</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6 · Highest priority for numerical risk assessment</em></p>\n<p>This question is especially useful because it requires you to understand the distinction between absolute risk and relative risk.</p>\n<p>The chapter defines standard deviation as dispersion around the average return, and coefficient of variation as relative dispersion useful for comparing investments with different average returns.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Arithmetic mean returns</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The formula for arithmetic mean return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\bar R=\\frac{\\sum R_t}{n}}$$</div>\n<p>For Security A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_A=\\frac{3+6+9+12+15}{5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\bar R_A=9\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For Security B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_B=\\frac{15+20+25+30+35}{5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\bar R_B=25\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Security B has a substantially higher historical average return.</p>\n<p>However, this alone does not determine whether it is superior.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Sample standard deviation</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The sample standard deviation is calculated as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ s=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum_{t=1}^{n}(R_t-\\bar R)^2}{n-1}} }$$</div>\n<p>The numerator measures the squared deviations of individual returns from their average.</p>\n<p>Dividing by $n-1$ produces sample variance.</p>\n<p>Taking the square root converts variance into standard deviation, expressed in the same return units as the original data.</p>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<p>Average return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_A=9\\%$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Return</th>\n<th>Deviation from 9%</th>\n<th>Squared deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3%</td>\n<td>-6</td>\n<td>36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>-3</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>+3</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>+6</td>\n<td>36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>90</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$s_A=\\sqrt{\\frac{90}{5-1}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{22.5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_A\\approx4.74\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<p>Average return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\bar R_B=25\\%$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Return</th>\n<th>Deviation from 25%</th>\n<th>Squared deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>-10</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>-5</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25%</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>+5</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>35%</td>\n<td>+10</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$s_B=\\sqrt{\\frac{250}{5-1}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{62.5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_B\\approx7.91\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Interpretation</p>\n<p>Security B has higher historical standard deviation, indicating that its returns were more dispersed around their mean.</p>\n<p>Therefore, based on the chapter's absolute volatility measure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_B&gt;s_A}$$</div>\n<p>Security B has greater measured absolute risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Coefficient of variation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The coefficient of variation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV=\\frac{s}{\\bar R}}$$</div>\n<p>For Security A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$CV_A=\\frac{4.7434}{9}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV_A\\approx0.527}$$</div>\n<p>For Security B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$CV_B=\\frac{7.9057}{25}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV_B\\approx0.316}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mean return</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample SD</td>\n<td>4.74%</td>\n<td>7.91%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CV</td>\n<td>0.527</td>\n<td>0.316</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Although Security B has greater standard deviation, it has a lower coefficient of variation.</p>\n<p>This means Security B exhibits less historical variability relative to its average return.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Critical evaluation</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Using standard deviation, Security A appears less risky because its historical returns deviate less from its average.</p>\n<p>Using the coefficient of variation, Security B appears more attractive in terms of variability relative to average return.</p>\n<p>This difference arises because Security B's mean return is substantially larger than Security A's.</p>\n<p>Thus, the investment ranking depends on the risk measure being used.</p>\n<p><strong>Comparing the two risk measures:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk measure</th>\n<th>Lower for</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Absolute volatility (SD)</td>\n<td><strong>Security A</strong></td>\n<td>SD = 4.74%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relative dispersion (CV)</td>\n<td><strong>Security B</strong></td>\n<td>CV = 0.316</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>However, selecting the superior investment requires additional consideration.</p>\n<p>First, historical returns do not guarantee future returns. A higher historical mean does not ensure that Security B will continue delivering 25%.</p>\n<p>Second, the sample contains only five observations, so the calculated statistics provide limited evidence about future uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Third, standard deviation treats positive and negative deviations as variability. Consequently, it does not distinguish favorable volatility from unfavorable volatility.</p>\n<p>Finally, the investor's required return and risk preferences must be considered.</p>\n<p>A highly risk-averse investor may prefer Security A's lower absolute volatility.</p>\n<p>Another investor may consider Security B more attractive because of its higher historical mean and lower relative dispersion, but that conclusion depends on whether the historical figures are reasonable estimates for the future.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Security A has lower absolute historical volatility, while Security B has lower relative dispersion. Neither can be declared universally superior without understanding the investor's objectives and the reliability of expected returns.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>Standard deviation and CV answer different questions.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma:\\text{ How much do returns fluctuate?}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{CV:\\text{ How much variability exists relative to the mean?}}$$</div>\n<p>A lower CV is useful for comparing positive average returns, but it is not a universal proof that an investment is better.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 6 - Integrated risk-return investment decision</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objectives 3 and 6</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Required returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter expresses required return as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{required}}=R_f+RP}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%$$</div>\n<p>Investment A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_A=4\\%+3\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_A=7\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Investment B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_B=4\\%+7\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_B=11\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Investment C:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_C=4\\%+14\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_C=18\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Required return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>7%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>11%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks for each calculation with appropriate substitution.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Evaluating acceptability</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The appropriate initial comparison is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_i)-R_{\\text{required},i}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8\\%-7\\%=+1\\text{ percentage point}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$12\\%-11\\%=+1\\text{ percentage point}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment C:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$16\\%-18\\%=-2\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Expected return compared with required return</strong> (hypothetical Chapter 4 investment alternatives):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">              0%        5%        10%       15%       20%\n              ├─────────┼─────────┼─────────┼─────────┤\n A  expected  ████████████████                           8%\n    required  ██████████████                             7%   → clears the hurdle (+1)\n B  expected  ████████████████████████                  12%\n    required  ██████████████████████                    11%   → clears the hurdle (+1)\n C  expected  ████████████████████████████████          16%\n    required  ████████████████████████████████████      18%   → fails the hurdle (-2)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Investments A and B meet their respective required-return hurdles.</p>\n<p>Investment C fails because its expected return is lower than the return required to compensate for its assessed risk.</p>\n<p>Although C has the highest expected return, it also has the highest standard deviation and a substantially higher required return.</p>\n<p>Therefore, C should not be preferred solely because its expected return is 16%.</p>\n<p>A and B both exceed their required returns by one percentage point.</p>\n<p>However, A has a standard deviation of only 6%, compared with B's 18%.</p>\n<p>This makes A a defensible choice for a moderately risk-averse investor, provided the risk premiums and expected returns were assessed reliably.</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, B might be appropriate for an investor willing to accept substantially higher volatility in pursuit of a higher expected return.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: A and B are acceptable based on their required-return hurdles, while C is not. The final choice between A and B depends on the investor's risk tolerance and confidence in the estimates.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investor risk preferences</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes three attitudes toward risk.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investor type</th>\n<th>Behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-averse</td>\n<td>Requires additional return to accept greater risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-indifferent</td>\n<td>Does not require a change in expected return as compensation for additional risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-seeking</td>\n<td>Is willing to accept lower return in exchange for greater risk, under the chapter's definition</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A risk-averse investor may accept greater risk, but only if adequately compensated.</p>\n<p>This is different from refusing all risky investments.</p>\n<p>A risk-indifferent investor does not increase required return when risk increases.</p>\n<p>A risk-seeking investor, as described by the chapter, is attracted to risk and may accept reduced expected compensation in exchange for greater risk.</p>\n<p>Most conventional investment evaluation is based on risk aversion, where higher required compensation accompanies greater risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Constructing the investment decision process</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter concludes by recommending four broad steps for combining risk and return.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Estimate expected returns:</strong> historical and projected returns, cash flows and valuation.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess investment risk:</strong> risk sources, standard deviation and coefficient of variation.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluate risk-return compensation:</strong> expected return compared with required return.</li>\n<li><strong>Select a suitable investment:</strong> the highest acceptable expected compensation consistent with investor risk preferences.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The investor should first verify the basis for the expected returns and risk premiums.</p>\n<p>The next step is to determine whether each investment's expected return is sufficient for the risk accepted.</p>\n<p>Alternatives that do not meet their required returns should generally be rejected under the chapter's framework.</p>\n<p>For alternatives meeting the hurdle, the investor should compare expected return with measured risk and select the opportunity best aligned with their willingness to tolerate uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Importantly, the provided information does not establish how the assets interact within a diversified portfolio, nor does it guarantee the accuracy of expected returns. A broader portfolio decision would require additional analysis.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investment A is a reasonable preliminary choice for the moderately risk-averse investor because it satisfies its required return while exhibiting substantially lower volatility than B. However, this is a conditional recommendation rather than proof that A will generate the best actual future result.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Additional short-answer questions for revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are suitable for shorter subjective questions worth approximately 5-10 marks. Try answering each before revealing the model response.</p>\n<p><strong>Q1. Differentiate historical, expected and required returns. Why should investors not confuse them?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Historical return measures what an investment earned in the past. Expected return is the investor's estimate of the return an investment will earn in the future. Required return is the minimum return the investor demands to be fully compensated for risk. Historical performance helps inform forecasts but does not guarantee future performance. An investment is potentially attractive when its expected return adequately satisfies its required return, subject to the assumptions and risks involved.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q2. Explain the difference between nominal and real returns.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Nominal return measures the rate of increase in monetary value without adjusting for price-level changes. Real return measures the change in purchasing power. Under the chapter's approximation, real return equals nominal return minus inflation. Thus an investor earning 8% while inflation is 5% achieves an approximate 3% real return. Nominal wealth can increase without an equally large improvement in purchasing power.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q3. Why is HPR less suitable for comparing investments with different holding periods?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> HPR measures the total return earned over an investment's holding period. It is easy to calculate and includes both income and capital appreciation, but it does not account for the time value of money or annualize returns. For example, gaining 30% in one year differs substantially from gaining 30% over five years. The chapter therefore uses compound annual yield or IRR to make return measurements more comparable across different investment horizons.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q4. How do issue characteristics and issuer characteristics influence the risk premium?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The chapter identifies risk premium as the additional return demanded for investment risk. Issue characteristics include the type, maturity and features of the security, while issuer characteristics concern the industry and individual company. Differences in these characteristics can affect uncertainty and therefore the compensation investors require. A security with greater perceived risks generally requires a higher risk premium.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q5. Differentiate purchasing-power risk from interest-rate risk.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Purchasing-power risk concerns changes in real investment value caused by inflation or deflation. For example, unexpected inflation reduces the purchasing power of a fixed coupon. Interest-rate risk concerns the effects of changing market interest rates on security prices or reinvestment opportunities. Existing fixed-rate bond prices generally decline when market yields rise. Although related, the risks operate through different mechanisms.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why is a lower standard deviation not enough to declare an investment superior?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Standard deviation measures dispersion of returns around their mean. A lower standard deviation indicates less measured volatility, but does not reveal whether the investment provides adequate expected return. For example, a low-volatility asset yielding 2% might be unsuitable for an investor requiring 8%. Investment selection must therefore consider the expected return, required return, investor risk preferences and other relevant constraints.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q7. Explain the use and limitation of the coefficient of variation.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The coefficient of variation measures standard deviation relative to average return, calculated as CV = σ/mean return. It helps compare investments whose positive average returns differ. A lower CV indicates less historical dispersion per unit of average return. However, CV becomes difficult to interpret when the mean is zero or negative, and it does not guarantee future risk-adjusted performance. Investors should use it together with other risk-return information.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q8. Evaluate the statement: 'Higher risk investments always produce higher returns.'</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The statement is incorrect. The chapter's risk-return trade-off indicates that investors generally require higher expected compensation for accepting greater uncertainty. It does not guarantee higher realized returns. High-risk investments may earn more, less or even produce substantial losses. The correct interpretation is that increased risk should be accompanied by increased required or expected compensation, not guaranteed profits.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Chapter 4 formula sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l4_required_stack\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Required return = real return + inflation premium + risk premium = risk-free rate + risk premium.</figcaption></figure></div><p>These are the main formulas you should recognize and apply.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital gain/loss</td>\n<td>$P_1-P_0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total return in currency</td>\n<td>$Income+(P_1-P_0)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holding-period return</td>\n<td>$\\frac{Income+(P_1-P_0)}{P_0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approximate real return</td>\n<td>Nominal return - inflation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-free rate</td>\n<td>Real return + expected inflation premium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Required return</td>\n<td>Risk-free rate + risk premium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lump-sum compound annual yield</td>\n<td>$\\left(\\frac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n}-1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash-flow stream IRR</td>\n<td>Solve $P_0=\\sum\\frac{CF_t}{(1+IRR)^t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compound annual growth rate</td>\n<td>$\\left(\\frac{Ending}{Beginning}\\right)^{1/n}-1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample standard deviation</td>\n<td>$s=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum(R_t-\\bar R)^2}{n-1}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coefficient of variation</td>\n<td>$CV=\\frac{s}{\\bar R}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For exam calculations, show the formula, substitution, numerical answer and interpretation. These four elements demonstrate much more understanding than a calculator answer alone.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. How to allocate marks in subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For an illustrative 20-mark evaluation question:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Suggested marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Accurate financial concepts and terminology</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explanation of underlying economic mechanisms</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application and correct calculations</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation, assumptions and justified conclusion</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is a practice framework, not an official marking scheme.</p>\n<p>For Chapter 4 in particular, the most frequent conceptual mistake is treating a calculated return as proof of investment attractiveness.</p>\n<p>Your lecturer may ask you to calculate a return worth four marks, then allocate another six marks to evaluating the result.</p>\n<p>Those additional marks are not awarded for calculating the same figure again. They require economic interpretation: whether the return meets the investor's hurdle, how uncertain the estimate is, and how the investment compares with alternatives.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. What I would prioritize for Chapter 4</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Highest priority - HPR, IRR and required return</p>\n<p>Practise calculating returns, distinguishing cumulative from annualized return and evaluating whether a proposed investment meets its required hurdle.</p>\n<p>Highest priority - Standard deviation and CV</p>\n<p>Understand how each is calculated, why the measures can produce different comparisons and why neither alone proves investment superiority.</p>\n<p>High priority - Sources of risk</p>\n<p>Master the nine risk categories and how they apply to unfamiliar scenarios. Focus particularly on distinguishing similar risk mechanisms.</p>\n<p>High priority - Risk-return trade-off</p>\n<p>Explain why additional risk demands additional expected compensation, without confusing expected returns with guaranteed outcomes.</p>\n<p>Important - Time value of money and growth</p>\n<p>Explain present-value investment decisions, compound annual growth and the reinvestment assumption associated with fully compounded returns.</p>\n<p>These priorities are based on the chapter's technical content and potential for higher-order examination questions, not verified information about the actual exam weighting.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Final higher-order examination exercise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Evaluate the following statement:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Investment A has a historical average return of 20%, while Investment B has a historical average return of 12%. Therefore, Investment A is superior and should be selected by every rational investor.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A complete response must establish several distinctions.</p>\n<p>First, historical return is evidence of past performance, not a guarantee of future return.</p>\n<p>Second, the historical average does not describe the variability surrounding the investment's returns. Investment A may have experienced significantly greater volatility than Investment B.</p>\n<p>Third, the investor's required return must be assessed. An expected return of 20% may still be inadequate if the investment requires greater compensation for risk.</p>\n<p>Fourth, suitability depends on the investor's attitude toward risk. A risk-averse investor may prefer a lower-return investment if it provides more appropriate compensation for the uncertainty accepted.</p>\n<p>Finally, the chapter recommends estimating expected return, measuring risk, evaluating whether the return is reasonable for the level of risk, and selecting investments consistent with the investor's risk preferences.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Highest Historical Return} \\neq \\text{Best Investment} }$$</div>\n<p>Instead:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Investment Decision} = f(\\text{Expected Return, Required Return, Risk, Investor Preference}) }$$</div>\n<p>The central lesson of Chapter 4 is that calculating return tells you what an investment earns or is expected to earn, but evaluating the relationship between return and risk tells you whether that investment is worth considering.</p>\n<p>That is the distinction to demonstrate consistently in your subjective examination.</p>\n<p>Source note: Prepared from your uploaded Chapter 4 - Return and Risk (Pearson, 2014). The risk classifications, financial concepts and return-decision framework follow the chapter. Numerical scenarios and marking allocations are original practice material, with some chapter examples adapted. The material does not substitute for Appendix 4A's dedicated time-value-of-money exercises or constitute verified past-exam questions.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4s-d10\">PART B - APPENDIX 4A: TIME VALUE OF MONEY</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Appendix 4A subjective overview</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Fundamentals of Investing · Return, Risk &amp; Time Value of Money</p>\n<p>The six Chapter 4 questions above are complemented by four more 20-mark questions covering the entirety of Appendix 4A. The combined examination preparation consists of:</p>\n<p><strong>Total questions:</strong> 10 · <strong>Total marks:</strong> 200 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 200 min</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chapter 4 - Questions 1-6 (120 marks):</strong> returns, HPR, IRR, required return, sources of risk, standard deviation, coefficient of variation and investment evaluation.</li>\n<li><strong>Appendix 4A - Questions 7-10 (80 marks):</strong> simple and compound interest, FV/PV, effective interest rates, ordinary annuities, mixed cash-flow streams, spreadsheet calculations and investment decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Appendix 4A portion follows your uploaded slides, including their examples of a RM1,000 deposit earning 8%, the eight-year annuity and the mixed-stream versus annuity comparison. I've adapted dollar-denominated examples into RM for practice where appropriate, without changing the financial principles.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. Additional subjective examination - Appendix 4A</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 7 - Simple interest, compound interest and compounding frequency (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor has RM5,000 available for a four-year investment.</p>\n<p>Three financial institutions offer the same nominal annual interest rate of 5%, but use different interest calculation methods.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Institution</th>\n<th>Interest method</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>5% simple interest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>5% compounded annually</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>5% compounded semiannually</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Differentiate simple interest and compound interest. Explain why compounding is economically significant.</p>\n<p>(b) [8 marks] Calculate the future value of RM5,000 after four years for each institution. Compare the results.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Calculate the effective annual interest rate for 5% nominal interest compounded semiannually and monthly.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Critically evaluate whether the investment with the most frequent compounding is necessarily the best investment.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 8 - Future value, present value and financial planning (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor is evaluating several financial goals.</p>\n<p>The available annual rate of return is assumed to be 8%, compounded annually.</p>\n<p>The investor wants to determine the value of money at different dates and whether current savings can finance a future financial obligation.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Explain the time value of money and distinguish future value from present value.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the future value of RM1,000 invested for two years and the present value of RM1,000 receivable in seven years.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] An investor must make a RM20,000 payment four years from today. Calculate the amount required today to finance this payment at 8%. Determine whether investing RM15,000 today would be sufficient.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate the assumptions underlying the calculations and explain how a spreadsheet can support time-value-of-money analysis.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 9 - Future value of an ordinary annuity (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A university graduate wishes to accumulate long-term savings.</p>\n<p>The graduate plans to deposit RM1,000 at the end of each year for eight years into an account earning 8% interest compounded annually.</p>\n<p>A financial advisor suggests that the investor should understand how the timing of deposits affects future wealth.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Define an annuity and an ordinary annuity. Explain why payment timing matters.</p>\n<p>(b) [8 marks] Calculate the future value of the eight-year ordinary annuity.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Suppose the investor could instead deposit RM8,000 immediately and leave it invested for eight years at the same rate. Calculate its future value.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate the differences between these saving strategies, including the practical limitations of comparing them based only on their ending balances.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 10 - Present value of annuities and mixed streams (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor is comparing two investment opportunities. Each requires an initial investment of RM195 and provides cash flows over five years.</p>\n<p>The cash flows correspond to the structures used in Appendix 4A, Table 4A.4.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>End of year</th>\n<th>Investment A: Mixed stream</th>\n<th>Investment B: Ordinary annuity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM30</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM40</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>RM70</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal total</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Assume both investments have comparable risk and the investor requires an 8% annual return.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Distinguish a mixed cash-flow stream from an ordinary annuity, explaining why this distinction matters for valuation.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the present value of Investment A's cash flows.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Calculate the present value of Investment B's cash flows. Compare the present values and evaluate both investments using the satisfactory-investment rule from Chapter 4.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Critically evaluate why two investments with identical nominal total cash flows can have different economic values.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Appendix 4A - Full model answers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Model Answer 7 - Simple interest and compounding</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Interest and compounding</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Simple versus compound interest</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Interest represents compensation paid by a borrower for using a lender's money.</p>\n<p>Under simple interest, interest is calculated only on the original principal. Previously earned interest does not generate additional interest.</p>\n<p>Under compound interest, interest is earned on both the original principal and accumulated interest from earlier periods.</p>\n<p>The distinction becomes increasingly important as the investment horizon increases.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Simple Interest}=P(1+rt)}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Compound Interest}=P(1+r)^t}$$</div>\n<p>The first formula produces linear growth, whereas the second produces compounded growth.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing the future values</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>Initial deposit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Annual nominal interest:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=5\\%$$</div>\n<p>Investment horizon:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$t=4\\text{ years}$$</div>\n<p>Institution A - Simple interest</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV_A=5{,}000(1+0.05(4))$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=5{,}000(1.20)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV_A=\\text{RM}6{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The investor earns RM250 interest each year based on the original RM5,000 principal.</p>\n<p>Institution B - Compounded annually</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV_B=5{,}000(1.05)^4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV_B=\\text{RM}6{,}077.53}$$</div>\n<p>The investment earns additional interest because accumulated interest becomes part of the account balance used to calculate subsequent interest.</p>\n<p>Institution C - Compounded semiannually</p>\n<p>Compounding occurs twice each year.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$m=2$$</div>\n<p>Periodic interest rate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r_p=\\frac{0.05}{2}=0.025$$</div>\n<p>Number of compounding periods:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$N=4(2)=8$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV_C=5{,}000(1.025)^8$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV_C=\\text{RM}6{,}092.01}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Institution</th>\n<th>Future value</th>\n<th>Total interest earned</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A - Simple</td>\n<td>RM6,000.00</td>\n<td>RM1,000.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B - Annual compounding</td>\n<td>RM6,077.53</td>\n<td>RM1,077.53</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C - Semiannual compounding</td>\n<td>RM6,092.01</td>\n<td>RM1,092.01</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Under the stated assumptions, Institution C produces the highest future value.</p>\n<p>The difference between B and C is only RM14.48. This demonstrates that more frequent compounding increases wealth, but the additional benefit may be relatively small.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Effective annual rate</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The effective annual rate (EAR) represents the actual annual compound return implied by the stated nominal interest rate and its compounding frequency.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{EAR=\\left(1+\\frac{r}{m}\\right)^m-1}$$</div>\n<p>For semiannual compounding:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EAR=\\left(1+\\frac{0.05}{2}\\right)^2-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{EAR=5.0625\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For monthly compounding:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EAR=\\left(1+\\frac{0.05}{12}\\right)^{12}-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{EAR\\approx5.1162\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The appendix's Table 4A.3 makes this same comparison using a 5% stated rate. It shows an increase from 5.000% with annual compounding toward approximately 5.127% with continuous compounding.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating the best investment</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Institution C provides the highest future value among the three specified choices, assuming identical risk, fees, liquidity, taxes and other terms.</p>\n<p>However, the investment offering the highest compounding frequency is not necessarily the best overall.</p>\n<p>For example, an investment with monthly compounding might impose withdrawal restrictions or additional fees that outweigh the relatively small interest benefit.</p>\n<p>Similarly, an investor who needs funds before maturity may place greater value on liquidity than on a marginally higher effective interest rate.</p>\n<p>Credit risk and the treatment of interest income can also affect the investment's attractiveness.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: More frequent compounding increases the effective return when the nominal annual rate is fixed and positive, but investment suitability must be evaluated using the complete terms of the investment rather than compounding frequency alone.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 8 - Future value and present value</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Lump-sum valuation and financial planning</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Time value of money</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The time value of money recognises that the value of money depends on when it is received.</p>\n<p>Money received earlier can be invested to generate returns, making it more valuable than an identical nominal amount received later under positive interest rates.</p>\n<p>Future value determines how much money invested today will grow to at a specified future date.</p>\n<p>Present value determines what a future cash flow is worth today, given an appropriate discount rate.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Future Value = Compounding Forward}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Present Value = Discounting Backward}}$$</div>\n<p>These calculations allow investors to compare financial amounts occurring at different dates.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Calculating FV and PV</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Future value of RM1,000 after two years</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=PV(1+r)^n$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=1{,}000(1.08)^2$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}1{,}166.40}$$</div>\n<p>This means RM1,000 invested today will grow to RM1,166.40 after two years at 8% compounded annually.</p>\n<p>Present value of RM1,000 receivable in seven years</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\frac{FV}{(1+r)^n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\frac{1{,}000}{(1.08)^7}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV=\\text{RM}583.49}$$</div>\n<p>This means RM583.49 invested today at 8% compounded annually would grow to approximately RM1,000 after seven years.</p>\n<p><strong>The time-value timeline</strong> (illustrative, for the two-year FV calculation):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">   t0                   t1                   t2\n   ├────────────────────┼────────────────────┤\n RM1,000 ──x1.08──► RM1,080 ──x1.08──► RM1,166.40     compounding: move FORWARD\n\n RM583.49 ◄────────── ÷ (1.08)^7 ─────────── RM1,000 at t7   discounting: move BACKWARD\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>(c) Financing a RM20,000 future payment</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investor requires:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=\\text{RM}20{,}000$$</div>\n<p>after:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=4\\text{ years}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Calculate the required initial deposit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV=\\frac{20{,}000}{(1.08)^4}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV=\\text{RM}14{,}700.60}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, approximately RM14,700.60 must be invested today to accumulate RM20,000 four years later at the assumed rate.</p>\n<p>The investor currently has RM15,000.</p>\n<p>Calculate its future value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=15{,}000(1.08)^4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}20{,}407.33}$$</div>\n<p>The amount exceeds the required payment by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$20{,}407.33-20{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}407.33}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, RM15,000 would be sufficient under the assumed 8% annual compound return.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation of assumptions and spreadsheet analysis</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The calculation assumes the 8% return can be earned consistently throughout the four-year period.</p>\n<p>It also assumes that no withdrawals reduce the invested principal and that relevant investment costs, taxes and inflation effects do not invalidate the financial objective.</p>\n<p>If the actual return is lower than 8%, the investor may fail to accumulate RM20,000.</p>\n<p>The projected surplus of RM407.33 is modest, so it should not be treated as substantial protection against uncertainty.</p>\n<p>The appendix explains that spreadsheet routines allow investors to change inputs and automatically recalculate financial values.</p>\n<p>For example, Excel can calculate:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Excel formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Required PV</td>\n<td><code>=PV(8%,4,0,-20000)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FV of RM15,000</td>\n<td><code>=FV(8%,4,0,-15000)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The negative numbers indicate the cash amounts paid or deposited by the investor, consistent with the financial-function sign convention.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Time-value calculations are essential for determining the capital required today to meet future obligations, but their usefulness depends on realistic estimates of investment returns and cash-flow timing.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 9 - Future value of an ordinary annuity</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Annuities and investment accumulation</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Annuity and ordinary annuity</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>An annuity is a series of equal cash flows occurring at equally spaced intervals.</p>\n<p>An ordinary annuity is a particular type of annuity in which each payment occurs at the end of the period.</p>\n<p>For example, depositing RM1,000 at the end of every year for eight years represents an ordinary annuity.</p>\n<p>The timing is important because different deposits earn interest for different lengths of time.</p>\n<p>The first deposit accumulates interest for seven years until the end of Year 8, whereas the final deposit earns no additional interest before the valuation date at the end of Year 8.</p>\n<p>Therefore, annuity valuation must account for the timing of each payment rather than simply multiplying the periodic deposit by the number of payments.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Future value of the annuity</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>Annual payment:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PMT=\\text{RM}1{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Annual interest:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Number of payments:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=8$$</div>\n<p>The future value of an ordinary annuity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ FV_{\\text{annuity}} = PMT\\left[\\frac{(1+r)^n-1}{r}\\right] }$$</div>\n<p>Substitute the data:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=1{,}000\\left[\\frac{(1.08)^8-1}{0.08}\\right]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=1{,}000(10.6366276)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}10{,}636.63}$$</div>\n<p>The total amount deposited is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8(\\text{RM}1{,}000)=\\text{RM}8{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, interest earned is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10{,}636.63-8{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}636.63}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Ordinary annuity accumulation:</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> total contributions   RM8,000.00  ████████████████████████\n accumulated interest  RM2,636.63  ████████\n                                   ────────────────────────────────\n ending value         RM10,636.63  ████████████████████████████████\n</code></pre>\n<p>The first deposit compounds for seven years and the eighth deposit occurs at the valuation date.</p>\n<p>The appendix uses this same eight-year, 8% ordinary-annuity example and obtains a future value of 10,636.63.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Investing RM8,000 immediately</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>If RM8,000 is invested at the beginning of the eight-year period, the entire amount earns interest for eight years.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$FV=8{,}000(1.08)^8$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{FV=\\text{RM}14{,}807.44}$$</div>\n<p>Compare this with the ordinary annuity:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment strategy</th>\n<th>Future value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM1,000 at each year-end for eight years</td>\n<td>RM10,636.63</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM8,000 deposited immediately</td>\n<td>RM14,807.44</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Difference</td>\n<td>RM4,170.81</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lump-sum investment produces greater future wealth because all RM8,000 earns compound interest for the full eight years.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation of the strategies</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Although the lump-sum investment generates a higher future value, it should not automatically be declared the superior investment strategy.</p>\n<p>The two strategies commit capital at different times.</p>\n<p>The lump-sum investor must provide the entire RM8,000 immediately, while the annuity investor contributes funds gradually.</p>\n<p>In fact, the present value of the eight RM1,000 year-end contributions, discounted at 8%, is approximately RM5,746.64.</p>\n<p>Thus, the two strategies do not require economically equivalent capital commitments at the beginning.</p>\n<p>A lump-sum strategy may benefit an investor who already has sufficient savings available.</p>\n<p>An ordinary-annuity saving plan may be more practical for an individual who accumulates investment capital through regular employment income.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Earlier investment generally produces more compound interest when the return is positive, but the appropriate saving strategy depends on capital availability, cash-flow requirements and the investor's financial circumstances.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 10 - Present value of mixed streams and annuities</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Cash-flow valuation and investment selection</em></p>\n<p>This question illustrates a particularly important financial principle:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Two investments can distribute exactly the same total amount of cash yet have different present values because the timing of those payments differs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>(a) Mixed streams versus ordinary annuities</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>A mixed cash-flow stream consists of payments that do not follow a uniform pattern.</p>\n<p>For example, an investment paying RM30, RM40, RM50, RM60 and RM70 over five years produces an unequal series of payments.</p>\n<p>An ordinary annuity consists of equal payments made at the end of regular periods.</p>\n<p>For example, receiving RM50 at the end of every year for five years represents an ordinary annuity.</p>\n<p>Both streams can be valued by discounting every cash flow individually.</p>\n<p>However, the annuity's uniform structure permits a simplified valuation formula.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Present value of Investment A</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Investment A pays:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Cash flow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>RM70</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Required return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>For an uneven stream:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} PV_A={}&amp;\\frac{30}{1.08} +\\frac{40}{1.08^2} +\\frac{50}{1.08^3}\\\\ &amp;+\\frac{60}{1.08^4} +\\frac{70}{1.08^5} \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p>Each cash flow contributes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Cash flow</th>\n<th>Present value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>RM30</td>\n<td>RM27.78</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>RM40</td>\n<td>RM34.29</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>RM50</td>\n<td>RM39.69</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td>RM44.10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>RM70</td>\n<td>RM47.64</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n<td>RM193.51</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Thus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_A\\approx \\text{RM}193.51}$$</div>\n<p>This reproduces the present-value result in Appendix 4A, Table 4A.5, using the source's five-year mixed cash-flow example.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Present value of Investment B and investment decision</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Investment B pays RM50 at the end of every year for five years.</p>\n<p>The present value of an ordinary annuity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ PV_{\\text{annuity}} = PMT\\left[\\frac{1-(1+r)^{-n}}{r}\\right] }$$</div>\n<p>Substitute:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PMT=\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r=8\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n=5$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_B = 50\\left[\\frac{1-(1.08)^{-5}}{0.08}\\right]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_B=\\text{RM}199.64}$$</div>\n<p>Now compare the two investments.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Investment A</th>\n<th>Investment B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal cash inflows</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n<td>RM250</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present value of inflows</td>\n<td>RM193.51</td>\n<td>RM199.64</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial cost</td>\n<td>RM195.00</td>\n<td>RM195.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NPV</td>\n<td>-RM1.49</td>\n<td>+RM4.64</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Chapter 4 satisfactory-investment rule is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV(\\text{Benefits})\\ge PV(\\text{Costs})}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NPV_A=193.51-195$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{NPV_A=-\\text{RM}1.49}$$</div>\n<p>For Investment B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NPV_B=199.64-195$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{NPV_B=+\\text{RM}4.64}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment</th>\n<th>NPV</th>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Decision</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td><strong>-RM1.49</strong></td>\n<td>NPV below zero</td>\n<td>Reject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td><strong>+RM4.64</strong></td>\n<td>NPV above zero</td>\n<td>Accept</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Based on the assumed 8% required return and equal initial cost, Investment A fails the satisfactory-investment test while Investment B satisfies it.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Investment B is the preferred alternative under the stated assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Why identical nominal payments have different values</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The two investments provide identical total nominal cash flows of RM250.</p>\n<p>However, their payment schedules differ.</p>\n<p>Investment A provides relatively small payments in the earlier years and larger payments later.</p>\n<p>Investment B provides consistent payments of RM50 from the first year onward.</p>\n<p>Earlier cash flows are more valuable because investors can receive and potentially reinvest them sooner.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the cash-flow timing produces a higher present value for Investment B.</p>\n<p>This difference can be evaluated mathematically through discounting.</p>\n<p>The result also depends on the chosen discount rate.</p>\n<p>For example, increasing the required return from 8% to 12% would reduce the present values of both investments, making neither satisfactory at an initial RM195 cost.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates that present-value decisions depend on both expected cash flows and the return required by the investor.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Investment B provides a higher present value because more of its cash flows arrive earlier. Investment evaluation must therefore consider the timing of benefits, not merely their nominal total.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Appendix 4A formula sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l4_annuity_vs_mixed\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Equal, evenly spaced, end-of-period payments allow the annuity shortcut; anything else is a mixed stream.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This supplements the Chapter 4 formula sheet I provided earlier.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple-interest future value</td>\n<td>$FV=PV(1+rn)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compound-interest future value</td>\n<td>$FV=PV(1+r)^n$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lump-sum present value</td>\n<td>$PV=\\frac{FV}{(1+r)^n}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FV with $m$ compounding periods/year</td>\n<td>$FV=PV(1+\\frac{r}{m})^{mn}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effective annual rate</td>\n<td>$EAR=(1+\\frac{r}{m})^m-1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future value of ordinary annuity</td>\n<td>$FV=PMT\\frac{(1+r)^n-1}{r}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present value of ordinary annuity</td>\n<td>$PV=PMT\\frac{1-(1+r)^{-n}}{r}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present value of mixed stream</td>\n<td>$PV=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Here, $r$ is the rate per period for ordinary annuity formulas, $n$ is the number of periods, and $m$ is the number of compounding periods per year for the nominal-rate formulas.</p>\n<p>The most important technical consideration is matching the interest-rate period with the number of periods.</p>\n<p>For example, a nominal annual rate of 12% compounded monthly means using a monthly periodic rate of 1%, not 12%, with 12 periods per year.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Choosing the appropriate formula in an exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Appropriate approach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM1,000 today grows for five years</td>\n<td>Lump-sum FV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM10,000 receivable in four years</td>\n<td>Lump-sum PV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM500 deposited every year</td>\n<td>FV of ordinary annuity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM500 received every year, valued today</td>\n<td>PV of ordinary annuity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM300, RM500, RM700 over successive years</td>\n<td>PV of mixed stream</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparing 5% nominal rates compounded differently</td>\n<td>EAR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deciding whether investment cash flows justify the price</td>\n<td>PV of benefits versus cost</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Don't choose the formula merely because certain numbers appear in the question. Start by identifying the cash-flow pattern and the date at which the investment must be valued.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Additional Appendix 4A short-answer practice</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Try answering these questions in your own words before revealing the explanations.</p>\n<p><strong>Q1. Why is RM1,000 received today normally worth more than RM1,000 received five years later?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Because money received today can be invested to earn a return over the five-year period. At a positive available rate, RM1,000 today can grow to more than RM1,000, while the future payment has not earned interest for the investor during that period. This is the opportunity-cost foundation of time value of money.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q2. Two investments both promise 8% nominal interest. One compounds annually; the other compounds quarterly. Is the quoted return sufficient to compare them?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Both state an 8% nominal annual rate, but quarterly compounding produces a higher effective annual rate under the same positive nominal rate. Calculate EAR before making a rate comparison and also examine risk, liquidity, fees and other investment conditions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q3. A financial calculator produces a negative PV when the investor expects a positive future cash receipt. Does that necessarily indicate a poor investment?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Financial calculators commonly apply a cash-flow sign convention. From the investor's perspective, money invested today is an outflow while a later receipt is an inflow. The negative sign can simply identify the direction of the cash flow, rather than a negative investment return.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q4. Why is an ordinary annuity different from an irregular income stream?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> An ordinary annuity has equal cash flows at equal intervals, with payments at period-end. An irregular stream has unequal or otherwise nonuniform cash flows and is typically valued by discounting individual payments. Both rely on present-value principles, but the annuity's regularity permits a simplified formula.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q5. Why can two investments have identical total nominal cash receipts yet different present values?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Because cash-flow timing matters. With a positive discount rate, payments received earlier have higher present values than identical payments received later. Investors should discount each cash flow to the same valuation date before comparing alternatives.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q6. An investment has positive NPV at an 8% required return. Would it necessarily remain satisfactory if the required return increased to 15%?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not necessarily. Increasing the discount rate reduces the present value of positive future cash flows. The investment may consequently move from positive to negative NPV. It must be recalculated at the revised required return rather than assumed to remain acceptable.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Combined Chapter 4 + 4A revision priorities</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now that both parts are included, this is how I would organize your preparation.</p>\n<p>Highest priority - Cash-flow valuation and return calculations</p>\n<p>Be able to calculate PV, FV, HPR, IRR, annuities and mixed streams. More importantly, know why each method is appropriate for a particular scenario.</p>\n<p>Highest priority - Investment acceptability</p>\n<p>Connect required return, discounting, NPV and IRR to determine whether an opportunity provides adequate compensation.</p>\n<p>High priority - Risk measurement</p>\n<p>Practise standard deviation and coefficient of variation, including interpreting why absolute and relative risk rankings may differ.</p>\n<p>High priority - Sources of risk and required compensation</p>\n<p>Identify the nine risk categories from Chapter 4 and explain how they affect the investor's required return.</p>\n<p>Important - Compounding and reinvestment</p>\n<p>Understand why earlier cash flows and more frequent compounding affect wealth accumulation, and distinguish nominal rates from effective returns.</p>\n<p>These priorities are based on the technical depth of the material, not confirmed examination weightings.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. The final integrated principle</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Chapter 4 and Appendix 4A work together because they address different parts of the same investment decision.</p>\n<p>Appendix 4A establishes how to value money at different points in time.</p>\n<p>Chapter 4 uses those principles to evaluate returns and determine whether the investment provides acceptable compensation for its risks.</p>\n<p>The combined decision process can be expressed as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Expected Cash Flows → Discount to Present Value → Compare with Investment Cost → Evaluate Return and Risk\"><span>Expected Cash Flows</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Discount to Present Value</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Compare with Investment Cost</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate Return and Risk</span></div>\n<p>A full-mark examination answer should explain not only how a financial value is calculated, but why that value matters for the investment decision and which assumptions could change the conclusion.</p>\n<p>You now have 10 subjective questions worth 200 marks, covering Chapter 4 and Appendix 4A, with the original six Chapter 4 questions retained and these four Appendix questions added.</p>\n<p>Source note: Appendix 4A exercises and worked examples are grounded in the uploaded The Time Value of Money slides (Pearson, 2014). The MYR scenarios, extended comparisons, spreadsheet formulas and marking allocations are adapted or constructed for examination practice.</p></div></details></div></details>");
